{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794522576336,"sessionID":"ses_e2e209366ffe3qZDn1hMKZPNUG","part":{"partID":"prt_1d1e172330023kWV1qDAs3wPr0","sessionID":"ses_e2e209366ffe3qZDn1hMKZPNUG","messageID":"msg_1d1e15b05001m11c56AZQYgBlH","type":"tool","id":"call_8ce1c7aa629248969170d192","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md, lines 1-583\n1: ---\n2: title: sk-code Surface Router — routing logic and resource maps\n3: description: First-class surface router document at the sk-code hub root (ROUTER.md). hub-router.json selects the workflow mode; this doc maps a request's code intent to the exact packet-local leaf resources that mode should load, emitting canonical (workflowMode, leafResourceId) pairs. Relocated from shared/references/smart-routing.md (v3.5.0.8) as the ROUTER.md consolidation pattern, with the approved sk-code shared-control delta (router self-reference removed, ten hub-shared paths normalized to shared/..., mapped shared controls declared).\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code smart routing\"\n6:   - \"surface resource maps\"\n7:   - \"intent classification routing\"\n8:   - \"load tier resource loading\"\n9:   - \"unknown surface fallback\"\n10: importance_tier: important\n11: contextType: general\n12: version: 2.3.19.0\n13: router_state: active\n14: skill_pointer: SKILL.md\n15: ---\n16: # sk-code Surface Router — routing logic and resource maps\n17: \n18: Leaf-set authority for sk-code. The routing authority is named once in `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING: `hub-router.json` is the mode vocabulary, and this document is the leaf-set router. Consolidates intent classification + resource loading into the single Barter-style smart-routing entry point. Detection logic lives in [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md); phase lifecycle lives in [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md).\n19: \n20: ---\n21: \n22: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n23: \n24: ### Purpose\n25: \n26: The smart router maps a detected surface (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) plus a classified intent (IMPLEMENTATION / DEBUGGING / VERIFICATION / etc.) to the exact references, assets, and verification commands needed for the active task. Loads only the smallest relevant set so the active context window stays focused.\n27: \n28: ### When to Use\n29: \n30: - After surface detection (see [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md)) determines the surface: a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN\n31: - When classifying task wording into the dominant work intent\n32: - When mapping intent to concrete reference paths\n33: - When picking verification commands for the surface\n34: \n35: ### Core Principle\n36: \n37: Routing is a two-stage decision: **surface-first, intent-second**. Surface narrows the resource family: a declared specific surface first, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN. Intent narrows the specific files within that family. The detected surface also goes to the compiled front door as `--surface-hint`. When a prompt ties between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads the bundle. SKILLED already ranks last, so a SKILLED hint changes nothing (`SKILL.md` §3).\n38: \n39: ### Key Sources\n40: \n41: - [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md): surface detection (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) and SKILLED language sub-detection\n42: - [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md) — Phase 1/2/3 lifecycle and phase-specific resource expectations\n43: - `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING — names the routing authority split and the compiled front door\n44: \n45: ---\n46: \n47: ## 2. INTENT MODEL\n48: \n49: Intent classification scores task text against weighted keyword signals to pick the dominant work type after surface detection. The vocabulary and weights are authored once, in the `INTENT_SIGNALS` block of §8 MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER; read them there. This section is the human-facing summary of how that block is scored, never a second copy of its keywords.\n50: \n51: ### Scoring algorithm\n52: \n53: 1. Sum weighted keyword hits from the request, target files, and known task context.\n54: 2. Boost explicit phase signals (verification, debugging, code quality) by +5 when the phase is unambiguous.\n55: 3. Select the top intent.\n56: 4. Select a second intent when the score delta to the next intent is small (`AMBIGUITY_DELTA = 1`).\n57: 5. For SKILLED surface, run language sub-detection after intent selection.\n58: \n59: ### Doc-only edit anti-signals\n60: \n61: Doc-only prose changes subtract from `sk-code` intent scoring and add to `sk-doc` scoring, even when the target file is under `.skilled/skills/`.\n62: \n63: | Prompt signal | Effect |\n64: | --- | --- |\n65: | `update headline`, `rewrite headline`, `headline section` | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n66: | `clarify description`, `clarify the X model`, `documentation section` | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n67: | `add a one-line summary`, `add summary at the top` | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n68: | `improve readme`, `reorganize readme`, `rewrite section` | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n69: | `SKILL.md` or `README.md` plus prose-only verbs | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n70: \n71: Do not apply this anti-signal when the same request asks to modify executable code, parser logic, tests, shell behavior, TypeScript/Python entrypoints, JSON schema behavior, or routing algorithms. In that case, keep SKILLED surface detection and load `sk-code`.\n72: \n73: Multi-symptom prompts like `debug the broken jsonc descriptor` should load both DEBUGGING and CONFIG. Prompts like `update TypeScript advisor fixture` should load SKILLED plus TYPESCRIPT and CODE_QUALITY.\n74: \n75: ### Surface-specific intent notes\n76: \n77: - **SKILLED** intent scoring favors language standards, hooks, config, scripts, advisor/tests, metadata, and alignment verification.\n78: - Unsupported surfaces stay UNKNOWN even if intent scoring is strong.\n79: \n80: ---\n81: \n82: ## 3. LOAD TIERS\n83: \n84: | Tier | When | Resources |\n85: | --- | --- | --- |\n86: | ALWAYS | Every invocation | The §8 `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble only: `shared/references/stack-detection.md`, `shared/references/phase-detection.md` and `shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md`. The style guide `sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md` loads with CODE_QUALITY, and the shared doctrine loads with its intent: `workflow-debug.md` with DEBUGGING and `workflow-verify.md` with VERIFICATION |\n87: | SURFACE | After SKILLED detection | Surface-specific resources (`sk-code-skilled/references/shared/*`) |\n88: | INTENT | After intent classification | Implementation, debugging, verification, testing, etc. matching the top-1 intent (and top-2 when ambiguous) |\n89: | LANGUAGE | SKILLED only | Split JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Shell, Rust, and Config standards from the matching `sk-code-skilled/references/<lang>/` resources |\n90: | ON_DEMAND | Explicit deep-dive keywords | Extended checklists and niche references (e.g. `sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md` for boundary templates) |\n91: \n92: ---\n93: \n94: ## 4. SKILLED MAP\n95: \n96: SKILLED loads from `sk-code-skilled/references/` and `sk-code-skilled/assets/`.\n97: \n98: ### Implementation shared tier\n99: \n100: IMPLEMENTATION loads the shared organization and naming baseline:\n101: \n102: - `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md`\n103: - `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md`\n104: \n105: ### Intent overlay\n106: \n107: | Intent | Resources |\n108: | --- | --- |\n109: | CODE_QUALITY | SKILLED only: `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md` plus the language-matching checklist, both when SKILLED is a resolved surface. A foreign surface loads the floor in `code-quality-standards.md` and its own declared rows |\n110: | VERIFICATION | `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/scripts/verify_alignment_drift.py` |\n111: | HOOKS | `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/hooks.md` |\n112: | CONFIG | `sk-code-skilled/references/config/*` |\n113: | LANGUAGE_STANDARDS | Detected language folder's `quick-reference.md`, `style-guide.md`, `quality-standards.md` |\n114: \n115: ### Language overlay\n116: \n117: | Language | Resources |\n118: | --- | --- |\n119: | JAVASCRIPT | `sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md` |\n120: | TYPESCRIPT | `sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md` |\n121: | PYTHON | `sk-code-skilled/references/python/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/python-checklist.md` |\n122: | SHELL | `sk-code-skilled/references/shell/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md` |\n123: | RUST | `sk-code-skilled/references/rust/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}/` (each split into topic parts), `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/` |\n124: | CONFIG | `sk-code-skilled/references/config/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/config-checklist.md` |\n125: \n126: ### Authoring checklist gating\n127: \n128: When SKILLED intent is `authoring-new-X`, additionally load the matching authoring checklist:\n129: \n130: | Authoring target | Checklist |\n131: | --- | --- |\n132: | New skill | `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md` |\n133: | New agent | `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md` |\n134: | New command | `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/command-authoring.md` |\n135: | New MCP server | `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md` |\n136: | Spec folder write | Now in system-spec-kit: `system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md` + `system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-write-recipe.md` |\n137: \n138: ### CI Workflow Map\n139: \n140: When the request names a GitHub Actions workflow, the hub bundles `sk-code-ci` from the request vocabulary and the `CI_WORKFLOW` intent loads its four rule references:\n141: \n142: | Resource | Carries |\n143: | --- | --- |\n144: | `sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md` | The rule index: path parity, SHA pins, README row, `set -euo pipefail`, fork and secret safety, matrix and `fail-fast` |\n145: | `sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md` | The twin rule, the rebuild-commit skip rule, push-only scope and the intentional exceptions table |\n146: | `sk-code-ci/references/job-shape-rules.md` | Timeouts, permissions, the concurrency template, the composite setup action and the switch `if:` shape |\n147: | `sk-code-ci/references/protected-set.md` | The repository CI profile and its keys, the workflows protected by function and the rules that follow |\n148: \n149: The map is the packet's own `RESOURCE_MAP` re-prefixed with `sk-code-ci/`, so the two stay equal.\n150: \n151: ### Frontend Map\n152: \n153: When a request asks about web frontend practice, or a Figma-to-code check, the hub bundles `sk-code-frontend` from request vocabulary alone:\n154: \n155: | Resource | Carries |\n156: | --- | --- |\n157: | `sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md` | The cross-stack core, token precedence, Figma boundary, and Frontend overrides contract |\n158: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md` | The web stack layer for markup, forms, layout, responsive behavior, media, and CSS architecture |\n159: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-elements-and-attributes.md` | Form autocomplete, dialog, and popover behavior |\n160: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-cascade-and-selectors.md` | Cascade layers and specificity conflicts |\n161: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-layout-and-sizing.md` | Container queries and stacking contexts |\n162: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-accessibility-patterns.md` | Accessibility patterns, visible focus, and live regions |\n163: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-performance-and-loading.md` | Web font loading and largest contentful paint |\n164: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-internationalization.md` | Right-to-left layout and bidirectional text |\n165: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-values.md` | Visual value decisions: spacing, type, weight, colour, shadow, radius, opacity, palettes and token sets |\n166: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-hierarchy.md` | Visual hierarchy and review: build procedure, symptom-to-fix tables, UX laws and the review pass |\n167: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-motion-and-interaction.md` | Motion and interaction: animation, input, touch, focus, performance and feedback rules |\n168: | `sk-code-frontend/references/figma-to-code.md` | The five-pass check for comparing a built screen with its Figma frame |\n169: \n170: The map is the packet's own `RESOURCE_MAP` for the web, visual and Figma-to-code intents, re-prefixed with `sk-code-frontend/`, so the two stay equal there.\n171: \n172: ---\n173: \n174: ## 5. VERIFICATION COMMANDS\n175: \n176: | Surface | Required verification evidence |\n177: | --- | --- |\n178: | SKILLED | `python3 .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/scripts/verify_alignment_drift.py --root <changed-scope>`; plus targeted language/project tests such as vitest, pytest, shellcheck, JSON validation, or spec validation for changed spec folders |\n179: | UNKNOWN | User-selected verification command set before completion claim |\n180: \n181: ---\n182: \n183: ## 6. UNKNOWN FALLBACK\n184: \n185: If no supported surface matches, ask:\n186: \n187: 1. Is this `.skilled/` system code, or an unsupported stack?\n188: 2. Which files or directories are changing?\n189: 3. Which verification command proves the claim?\n190: 4. Should a new `sk-code` route be planned before implementation?\n191: \n192: Canonical sk-code owns the SKILLED surface, the `sk-code-ci` GitHub Actions surface and the stack-agnostic `sk-code-frontend` surface for web practice. Go, Next.js server code and React Native surfaces live in a project fork.\n193: \n194: ### UNKNOWN fallback checklist\n195: \n196: Returned when intent confidence is low (`max(intent_scores) < 0.5`) OR when the user explicitly asks for stack-agnostic guidance:\n197: \n198: - Confirm the detected surface (a declared specific surface, the SKILLED fallback or UNKNOWN)\n199: - Confirm the task intent (implementation / debugging / verification / etc.)\n200: - Provide one concrete input, error, or expected output\n201: - Confirm the verification command set before completion\n202: \n203: ---\n204: \n205: ## 7. LOADING DISCIPLINE (DO / DON'T)\n206: \n207: ### DO\n208: \n209: - Load only references needed for current intent + surface\n210: - Use exact canonical file paths in playbook result YAML (never directory placeholders)\n211: - Cite surface + top-1 intent + loaded resource paths + verification command in your final answer\n212: \n213: ### DON'T\n214: \n215: - Load all of `sk-code-skilled/` indiscriminately (\"just in case\")\n216: - Load Go, Next.js server code or React Native resources from canonical sk-code; those live in a project fork\n217: - Drop directory placeholders in playbook result YAML\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 8. MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER (replay / benchmark source)\n222: \n223: This block is the authored source for the intent vocabulary and weights. §2 explains how it is scored and does not repeat the keywords; §4 renders the same rows as prose. When an intent row changes here, update the matching prose row in §4.\n224: \n225: This projection is intentionally lossy in two documented ways the flat dictionary cannot express, both enforced by the prose contract and the surface-detection pseudocode in this file's §11:\n226: \n227: - **No phase boosts.** The `+5` unambiguous-phase boost from the §2 scoring algorithm is not represented; every keyword carries unit weight here.\n228: - **No doc-only anti-signals.** The `sk-code -2 / sk-doc +3` anti-signals (§2) that route prose-only edits away from this skill are a pre-filter the flat model does not carry.\n229: \n230: ```python\n231: # Always-loaded routing preamble: every code task detects stack, detects phase,\n232: # consults the router, and applies the universal quality baseline before any\n233: # surface-specific resource. These are loaded on every route.\n234: DEFAULT_RESOURCE = [\n235:     \"shared/references/stack-detection.md\",\n236:     \"shared/references/phase-detection.md\",\n237:     \"shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md\",\n238: ]\n239: \n240: INTENT_SIGNALS = {\n241:     \"IMPLEMENTATION\":     {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"implement\", \"build\", \"create\", \"feature\", \"component\", \"module\", \"authoring\"]},\n242:     \"CODE_QUALITY\":       {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"lint\", \"format\", \"quality gate\", \"naming\", \"standards\", \"code smell\", \"comment hygiene\", \"restraint\", \"yagni\", \"simplify\", \"over-engineering\", \"bloat\", \"skipped_untrusted\", \"untrusted repository check\", \"trustrepochecks\", \"quality config\", \"repository/quality.json\", \"configured check\", \"unverified\"]},\n243:     \"DEBUGGING\":          {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"debug\", \"broken\", \"failing\", \"stack trace\", \"console error\", \"regression\"]},\n244:     \"VERIFICATION\":       {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"verify\", \"passing\", \"type-check\", \"alignment drift\", \"completion claim\"]},\n245:     \"HOOKS\":              {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"session-prime\", \"user-prompt-submit\", \"pre-tool-use\", \"post-tool-use\"]},\n246:     \"CONFIG\":             {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"jsonc\", \".json\", \".jsonc\", \"descriptor\", \"config schema\"]},\n247:     \"JAVASCRIPT\":         {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"javascript\", \"vanilla js\", \".js\", \"commonjs\", \".cjs\", \".mjs\"]},\n248:     \"TYPESCRIPT\":         {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"typescript\", \".ts\", \".tsx\"]},\n249:     \"PYTHON\":             {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"python\", \".py\", \"docstring\"]},\n250:     \"SHELL\":              {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"shell script\", \"bash\", \".sh\"]},\n251:     \"RUST\":               {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"rust\", \".rs\", \"cargo.toml\", \"cargo.lock\", \"napi-rs\", \"napi_rs\", \"#[napi]\", \"wasm-bindgen\", \"wasm_bindgen\", \"#[wasm_bindgen]\", \"wasi\", \"cdylib\"]},\n252:     \"CI_WORKFLOW\":        {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"github actions workflow\", \"ci workflow\", \"github actions yaml\", \"workflow path filters\", \"github workflow timeout\", \"github workflow concurrency\", \"github workflow permissions\", \"required status check workflow\", \"ci job\", \"action to a sha\", \"sha-pinned action\", \"github actions permissions\", \"ci workflows\", \"ci switch\", \"turn off ci\", \".github/workflows\", \"workflow concurrency\", \"github actions release workflow\", \"github actions deploy workflow\", \"release workflow yaml\", \"deploy workflow yaml\", \"github actions minutes\", \"actions minutes\", \"actions runner minutes\", \"runner minutes\", \"ci workflow cost\", \"ci cost\", \"reduce ci costs\", \"lower ci costs\", \"cut ci costs\", \"reduce our ci costs\", \"lower our ci costs\", \"ci spend\", \"ci bill\", \"ci minutes cost\", \"cut the ci minutes\", \"cut ci minutes\", \"reduce ci minutes\", \"ci pipeline cost\", \"ci pipeline cheaper\", \"ci pipelines cheaper\", \"cheaper ci pipeline\", \"ci runs cheaper\", \"failing ci\", \"failing ci pipeline\", \"ci failure\", \"ci failures\", \"ci is failing\", \"broken ci\", \"debug the ci\", \"debug ci\", \"cost of our ci\", \"make ci cheaper\", \"github actions bill\", \"github actions cost\", \"turn off the ci\", \"turn on the ci\", \"ci review workflow\"]},\n253:     \"FRONTEND_WEB\":        {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"semantic html markup\"]},\n254:     \"WEB_ELEMENTS\":        {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"html form autocomplete\", \"dialog element\", \"popover attribute\"]},\n255:     \"WEB_CASCADE\":         {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"css cascade layers\", \"css specificity conflict\"]},\n256:     \"WEB_LAYOUT\":          {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"container query layout\", \"css stacking context\"]},\n257:     \"WEB_A11Y\":            {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"web accessibility pattern\", \"focus visible styling\", \"keyboard focus\", \"broken tab order\", \"accessible name\", \"aria live region\", \"focus ring accessibility\", \"focus outline removed\", \"screen reader support\", \"aria label missing\", \"alt text missing\", \"wcag audit\"]},\n258:     \"WEB_PERF\":            {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"web font loading\", \"largest contentful paint\"]},\n259:     \"WEB_I18N\":            {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"right to left layout\", \"bidirectional text\"]},\n260:     \"WEB_VISUAL_VALUES\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"what padding should this have\", \"how much padding\", \"what font size should this be\", \"spacing between elements\", \"spacing scale\", \"type scale\", \"build a color palette\", \"shade ramp\", \"box shadow elevation\", \"border radius consistency\", \"contrast ratio failure\", \"grey text on colored background\", \"dark mode palette\", \"concentric border radius\", \"layered shadows\", \"shadow color on dark surface\", \"tabular numbers\", \"what should this button look like\", \"colour decision\", \"color decision\", \"padding\", \"type scale for\", \"font size\", \"colour palette\", \"color palette\", \"contrast ratio\", \"corner radius\", \"box shadow\", \"spacing between\", \"contrast of\", \"check the contrast\", \"colour contrast\", \"color contrast\", \"muted text\"]},\n261:     \"WEB_VISUAL_HIERARCHY\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"ui looks off\", \"looks amateur\", \"feels cluttered\", \"make this look better\", \"make this ui look designed\", \"visual hierarchy\", \"no visual hierarchy\", \"nothing draws the eye\", \"everything competes for attention\", \"everything looks the same\", \"emphasize by de-emphasizing\", \"how to style a destructive button\", \"this page feels overwhelming\", \"where do i start designing\", \"design a new screen from scratch\", \"how much white space\", \"why does this look wrong\", \"does this look right\", \"critique this design\", \"design critique\", \"visual audit\", \"design review findings\", \"design review\", \"review this design\", \"review this screen\", \"ux laws\", \"cognitive load\", \"progressive disclosure\", \"empty state design\", \"look wrong\", \"looks wrong\", \"looks off\", \"visual review\", \"type hierarchy\"]},\n262:     \"WEB_MOTION\":        {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"animation duration\", \"transition duration\", \"easing curve\", \"spring versus easing\", \"twelve principles of animation\", \"when not to animate\", \"response time budget\", \"touch target size\", \"hover state\", \"active pressed state\", \"disabled state styling\", \"button states\", \"animation\", \"transition timing\", \"easing\", \"hover\", \"focus-visible\", \"interaction states\"]},\n263:     \"FIGMA_TO_CODE\":      {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"figma to code\", \"check built screen against figma\", \"compare build with figma frame\", \"figma frame\", \"figma frame check\", \"implement a figma design\"]},\n264: }\n265: \n266: RESOURCE_MAP = {\n267:     \"IMPLEMENTATION\": [\n268:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md\",\n269:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/organization-security-and-examples.md\",\n270:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md\",\n271:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/code-organization/imports-and-exports.md\",\n272:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/code-organization/directory-and-test-conventions.md\",\n273:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md\",\n274:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/command-authoring.md\",\n275:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md\",\n276:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md\",\n277: \n278:     ],\n279:     \"CODE_QUALITY\": [\n280:         \"shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md\",\n281:         \"shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md\",\n282:         \"sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md\",\n283:         \"sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md\",\n284:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md\",  # SKILLED only: the house checklist loads when SKILLED resolves, never for a foreign surface\n285:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md\",\n286:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md\",\n287:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md\",\n288:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md\",\n289:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md\",\n290:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/python-checklist.md\",\n291:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md\",\n292:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md\",\n293:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md\",\n294:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md\",\n295:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md\",\n296:         \"sk-code-review/assets/code-quality-checklist.md\",\n297: \n298:     ],\n299:     \"DEBUGGING\": [\n300:         \"shared/references/workflow-debug.md\",\n301: \n302:     ],\n303:     \"VERIFICATION\": [\n304:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md\",\n305:         \"shared/references/workflow-verify.md\",\n306:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/scripts/README.md\",\n307: \n308:     ],\n309:     \"HOOKS\": [\n310:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/hooks.md\",\n311:     ],\n312:     \"CONFIG\": [\n313:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/config/style-guide.md\",\n314:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/config/quality-standards.md\",\n315:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/config/quick-reference.md\",\n316:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/config-checklist.md\",\n317:     ],\n318:     \"JAVASCRIPT\": [\n319:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/style-guide.md\",\n320:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md\",\n321:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md\",\n322:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quick-reference.md\",\n323:     ],\n324:     \"TYPESCRIPT\": [\n325:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/style-guide/overview-strict-and-naming.md\",\n326:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/style-guide/formatting-imports-and-coexistence.md\",\n327:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quality-standards/overview-and-type-system.md\",\n328:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quality-standards/tsdoc-errors-and-async.md\",\n329:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quality-standards/tsconfig-and-modules.md\",\n330:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quick-reference/template-naming-and-types.md\",\n331:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quick-reference/imports-errors-and-tsconfig.md\",\n332:     ],\n333:     \"PYTHON\": [\n334:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md\",\n335:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md\",\n336:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/python/quick-reference.md\",\n337:     ],\n338:     \"SHELL\": [\n339:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/style-guide/overview-structure-and-naming.md\",\n340:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/style-guide/variables-functions-and-output.md\",\n341:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/quality-standards/overview-and-priority-blockers.md\",\n342:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/quality-standards/validation-security-and-shellcheck.md\",\n343:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/quick-reference/template-variables-and-loops.md\",\n344:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/quick-reference/functions-strings-and-checklist.md\",\n345:     ],\n346:     \"RUST\": [\n347:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/overview-and-file-header.md\",\n348:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/toolchain-and-project-structure.md\",\n349:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md\",\n350:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/formatting-and-imports.md\",\n351:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/commenting-and-rustdoc.md\",\n352:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/interop-model.md\",\n353:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/interop-errors-and-parity.md\",\n354:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/overview-and-data-ownership.md\",\n355:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/modeling-collections-and-api.md\",\n356:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/docs-errors-and-async.md\",\n357:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/build-and-organization.md\",\n358:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/determinism-and-parity.md\",\n359:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md\",\n360:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/naming-ordering-and-signatures.md\",\n361:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/collections-imports-and-errors.md\",\n362:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/rustdoc-and-cargo.md\",\n363:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/determinism-parity-and-related.md\",\n364:     ],\n365:     \"CI_WORKFLOW\": [\n366:         \"sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md\",\n367:         \"sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md\",\n368:         \"sk-code-ci/references/job-shape-rules.md\",\n369:         \"sk-code-ci/references/protected-set.md\",\n370:     ],\n371:     \"FRONTEND_WEB\": [\n372:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n373:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n374:     ],\n375:     \"WEB_ELEMENTS\": [\n376:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n377:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n378:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-elements-and-attributes.md\",\n379:     ],\n380:     \"WEB_CASCADE\": [\n381:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n382:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n383:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-cascade-and-selectors.md\",\n384:     ],\n385:     \"WEB_LAYOUT\": [\n386:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n387:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n388:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-layout-and-sizing.md\",\n389:     ],\n390:     \"WEB_A11Y\": [\n391:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n392:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n393:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-accessibility-patterns.md\",\n394:     ],\n395:     \"WEB_PERF\": [\n396:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n397:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n398:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-performance-and-loading.md\",\n399:     ],\n400:     \"WEB_I18N\": [\n401:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n402:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n403:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-internationalization.md\",\n404:     ],\n405:     \"WEB_VISUAL_VALUES\": [\n406:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n407:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-values.md\",\n408:     ],\n409:     \"WEB_VISUAL_HIERARCHY\": [\n410:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n411:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-hierarchy.md\",\n412:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-values.md\",\n413:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-accessibility-patterns.md\",\n414:     ],\n415:     \"WEB_MOTION\": [\n416:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n417:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-motion-and-interaction.md\",\n418:     ],\n419:     \"FIGMA_TO_CODE\": [\n420:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/figma-to-code.md\",\n421:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n422:     ],\n423: }\n424: \n425: # Hub-level shared controls, declared once here. This list holds every\n426: # contained shared/ path that RESOURCE_MAP references but that is exempt from\n427: # typed-leaf projection, because it has no single packet owner. The root-router\n428: # contract validates each entry and requires RESOURCE_MAP to reference it.\n429: # DEFAULT_RESOURCE above is the other half of the set: its always-loaded\n430: # preamble paths are hub-level controls too. No other shared/ path is a\n431: # control, so a guard that needs the control set reads these two lists.\n432: SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES = [\n433:     \"shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md\",\n434:     \"shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md\",\n435:     \"shared/references/workflow-debug.md\",\n436:     \"shared/references/workflow-verify.md\",\n437: ]\n438: ```\n439: \n440: ### Surface-aware loading (route-time)\n441: \n442: The router does NOT load the whole matched-intent union. After surface detection (§1, `stack-detection.md`), a route loads:\n443: \n444: - the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble (stack/phase detection and the universal quality baseline), plus\n445: - the `shared/` entries the matched intents map (for example `code-quality-standards.md` under CODE_QUALITY, `workflow-debug.md` under DEBUGGING and `workflow-verify.md` under VERIFICATION), never the whole `shared/references/universal/` folder, plus\n446: - only the **detected surface's** slice (`<surface>/references/*`) for the matched intents.\n447: \n448: - It does not load another surface's resources.\n449: - It defers `assets/*` (checklists, recipes, templates) to on-demand rather than the first slice.\n450: - Within SKILLED it slices once more by the **detected language** (§1 sub-detection): a TypeScript task loads `sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/*` plus the language-agnostic `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/*`, not the Python, shell, config, or JavaScript folders.\n451: - An `UNKNOWN` surface falls back to the preamble plus the shared-tier entries its matched intents map.\n452: \n453: This is what stops a routine single-surface task from pulling the full cross-surface set. The deterministic router-replay enforces the same rule, so the benchmark measures it.\n454: \n455: ---\n456: \n457: ## 9. HOW TO READ THIS\n458: \n459: - Every code task first loads the always-on preamble (stack + phase detection and the universal quality baseline); routing then narrows to the detected surface's slice for the matched intents.\n460: - One dominant intent on one surface loads that surface's slice; two near-tied intents (within the router's ambiguity delta) load both intent sets, deduped by canonical pair.\n461: - A single-surface task never pulls another surface's resources.\n462: - No keyword match, or an `UNKNOWN` surface, falls back to the preamble plus the shared-tier entries its matched intents map — confirm the surface and intent before loading more. See §6 (UNKNOWN FALLBACK) and §7 (LOADING DISCIPLINE) for the full rules.\n463: \n464: ---\n465: \n466: ## 10. RELATED RESOURCES\n467: \n468: - [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md): surface detection (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) + SKILLED language sub-detection\n469: - [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md) — Phase 1/2/3 lifecycle and per-phase resource loading\n470: - `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING — names the routing authority split and the compiled front door\n471: - Barter equivalent: `barter/.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/smart-routing.md` (different routing key — git-remote project — but same structural pattern)\n472: \n473: ---\n474: \n475: ## 11. MACHINE ROUTER\n476: \n477: The hub's detailed routing prose, moved here from `SKILL.md` so the skill body keeps only a short routing summary and a pointer to this file. It sits last, after the stage-two map above, and it declares no map of its own: the `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP` block in section 8 stays the only replay source.\n478: \n479: > **Compiled routing (default-on, flag-gated, additive).** Resolve the mode via the compiled router contract first:\n480: > ```bash\n481: > node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"\n482: > ```\n483: > Follow the returned decision: `route` (use its `targets`), `clarify`/`defer` (disambiguate) or `reject` (refuse). On a `{\"servingAuthority\":\"legacy\"}` sentinel or any error, use the routing below. The front door self-gates on serving-authority. Compiled routing is now the default for `sk-code`. Set `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING=0`, the explicit kill-switch, to force legacy routing fleet-wide.\n484: \n485: **Session surface hint.** Before you call the front door, run the surface detection in [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md) on the session's working directory and the files it is editing.\n486: Then add its result as `--surface-hint <SURFACE>`, for example `--surface-hint SKILLED`.\n487: When the prompt's keywords tie between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads them.\n488: Workflow modes keep first place, and the targets and the bundle kind stay the same.\n489: `SKILLED` is the fallback surface and always ranks last, so a `SKILLED` hint changes nothing.\n490: With `UNKNOWN`, no hint or a surface the prompt did not match, the `routerPolicy.tieBreak` order applies.\n491: \n492: ### Surface Router: per-intent leaf sets\n493: \n494: Root `ROUTER.md` owns the second-stage `INTENT_SIGNALS` / `RESOURCE_MAP`, while packet-owned resources stay typed through `leaf-manifest.json`.\n495: The hub-level shared controls are declared once, in `ROUTER.md` §8: the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list plus the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble. They resolve on disk but never project as leaves.\n496: The hub router keeps stage-one mode selection and its `shared/README.md` fallback.\n497: \n498: ### The discriminator\n499: - **`workflowMode`** - the public mode/packet key: `sk-code-quality`, `sk-code-review` (workflow) or `sk-code-ci`, `sk-code-frontend`, `sk-code-skilled` (surface).\n500: - **`packetKind`** - the axis: `workflow` (a mode that acts) or `surface` (read-only evidence bundled alongside a mode).\n501: - **`backendKind`** - which backend runs the packet: `surface-router` or `review-cache` for workflow modes, `evidence-base` for surface packets.\n502: \n503: Surface packets are advisor-invisible (`routingClass: metadata`, read-only `toolSurface`).\n504: The advisor still routes the single identity `sk-code`, and the hub bundles zero-or-more surfaces as evidence via `routerPolicy.outcomes.surfaceBundle`.\n505: The workflow mode comes first, followed by surfaces.\n506: \"review my typescript module for jank\" → `[sk-code-review, sk-code-skilled]`.\n507: Bundled surfaces follow the `routerPolicy.tieBreak` order. It ranks `sk-code-frontend` ahead of `sk-code-ci`, then `sk-code-skilled` last, because a new surface goes ahead of the existing ones and the SKILLED fallback stays last.\n508: \n509: ### Surface detection\n510: \n511: Surface detection lives once in the hub's `shared/` layer and every mode and surface consumes it.\n512: It tries every declared specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n513: When a specific surface and `SKILLED` both match, the specific one leads and `SKILLED` comes along as second evidence.\n514: Each surface declares its markers in a `surfaceDetection` block in `mode-registry.json`, or in its own `graph-metadata.json` when it lives outside the hub.\n515: Modes own workflow contracts, not surface identity.\n516: `sk-code-ci` and `sk-code-frontend` are the surfaces that stack detection does not select. The hub bundles both from request vocabulary alone: CI through `code-ci-aliases` and `code-ci-runtime`, and Frontend through `code-frontend-aliases` in `hub-router.json`.\n517: \n518: ### Fallback route\n519: \n520: When no workflow mode dominates (a bare implement/debug/verify request), the router defers to a pure surface bundle: it detects the surface, loads that surface's evidence and workflow doctrine, and the agent acts. `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`, so the hub does not force a stale default. An unclear code intent asks for disambiguation.\n521: \n522: Per-mode behavior is **not flattened**: each packet keeps its own code-work contract, standards, evidence rules, and tool-permission guards.\n523: \n524: ### Routing rule\n525: ```python\n526: from pathlib import Path\n527: \n528: SKILL_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n529: UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST = [\n530:     \"Confirm whether this is sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend or sk-code-skilled work\",\n531:     \"Confirm the target files or runtime surface\",\n532:     \"Confirm the expected action: implement, debug, verify, quality gate, or review\",\n533:     \"Confirm the verification command set before completion\",\n534: ]\n535: \n536: def _guard_in_skill(relative_path: str) -> str:\n537:     resolved = (SKILL_ROOT / relative_path).resolve()\n538:     resolved.relative_to(SKILL_ROOT)\n539:     if resolved.name != \"SKILL.md\" and resolved.suffix.lower() != \".md\":\n540:         raise ValueError(f\"Only markdown skill resources are routable: {relative_path}\")\n541:     return resolved.relative_to(SKILL_ROOT).as_posix()\n542: \n543: def load_if_available(relative_path: str, loaded: list[str], seen: set[str]) -> None:\n544:     guarded = _guard_in_skill(relative_path)\n545:     path = SKILL_ROOT / guarded\n546:     if path.exists() and guarded not in seen:\n547:         load(guarded)\n548:         loaded.append(guarded)\n549:         seen.add(guarded)\n550: \n551: def route(task):\n552:     loaded, seen = [], set()\n553:     registry = read_json(SKILL_ROOT / \"mode-registry.json\")\n554:     workflow_mode = explicit_mode_hint(task) or classify_workflow_mode(task)\n555: \n556:     if not workflow_mode:\n557:         load_if_available(\"shared/README.md\", loaded, seen)\n558:         return {\n559:             \"load_level\": \"UNKNOWN_FALLBACK\",\n560:             \"needs_disambiguation\": True,\n561:             \"disambiguation_checklist\": UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST,\n562:             \"resources\": loaded,\n563:         }\n564: \n565:     entry = find_registry_entry(registry, workflowMode=workflow_mode)\n566:     if not entry:\n567:         load_if_available(\"shared/README.md\", loaded, seen)\n568:         return {\"load_level\": \"UNKNOWN_FALLBACK\", \"unknown_mode\": workflow_mode, \"resources\": loaded}\n569: \n570:     packet_skill = f\"{entry['packet']}/SKILL.md\"\n571:     load_if_available(packet_skill, loaded, seen)\n572: \n573:     for surface_mode in surface_bundle_from_hub_router(task):\n574:         surface_entry = find_registry_entry(registry, workflowMode=surface_mode)\n575:         if surface_entry:\n576:             load_if_available(f\"{surface_entry['packet']}/SKILL.md\", loaded, seen)\n577: \n578:     if not loaded:\n579:         load_if_available(\"shared/README.md\", loaded, seen)\n580:         return {\"load_level\": \"UNKNOWN_FALLBACK\", \"notice\": \"matched registry but no packet SKILL.md was available\", \"resources\": loaded}\n581: \n582:     return {\"workflowMode\": workflow_mode, \"backendKind\": entry[\"backendKind\"], \"resources\": loaded}\n583: ```","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md, lines 1-583\n1: ---\n2: title: sk-code Surface Router — routing logic and resource maps\n3: description: First-class surface router document at the sk-code hub root (ROUTER.md). hub-router.json selects the workflow mode; this doc maps a request's code intent to the exact packet-local leaf resources that mode should load, emitting canonical (workflowMode, leafResourceId) pairs. Relocated from shared/references/smart-routing.md (v3.5.0.8) as the ROUTER.md consolidation pattern, with the approved sk-code shared-control delta (router self-reference removed, ten hub-shared paths normalized to shared/..., mapped shared controls declared).\n4: trigger_phrases:\n5:   - \"sk-code smart routing\"\n6:   - \"surface resource maps\"\n7:   - \"intent classification routing\"\n8:   - \"load tier resource loading\"\n9:   - \"unknown surface fallback\"\n10: importance_tier: important\n11: contextType: general\n12: version: 2.3.19.0\n13: router_state: active\n14: skill_pointer: SKILL.md\n15: ---\n16: # sk-code Surface Router — routing logic and resource maps\n17: \n18: Leaf-set authority for sk-code. The routing authority is named once in `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING: `hub-router.json` is the mode vocabulary, and this document is the leaf-set router. Consolidates intent classification + resource loading into the single Barter-style smart-routing entry point. Detection logic lives in [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md); phase lifecycle lives in [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md).\n19: \n20: ---\n21: \n22: ## 1. OVERVIEW\n23: \n24: ### Purpose\n25: \n26: The smart router maps a detected surface (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) plus a classified intent (IMPLEMENTATION / DEBUGGING / VERIFICATION / etc.) to the exact references, assets, and verification commands needed for the active task. Loads only the smallest relevant set so the active context window stays focused.\n27: \n28: ### When to Use\n29: \n30: - After surface detection (see [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md)) determines the surface: a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN\n31: - When classifying task wording into the dominant work intent\n32: - When mapping intent to concrete reference paths\n33: - When picking verification commands for the surface\n34: \n35: ### Core Principle\n36: \n37: Routing is a two-stage decision: **surface-first, intent-second**. Surface narrows the resource family: a declared specific surface first, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN. Intent narrows the specific files within that family. The detected surface also goes to the compiled front door as `--surface-hint`. When a prompt ties between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads the bundle. SKILLED already ranks last, so a SKILLED hint changes nothing (`SKILL.md` §3).\n38: \n39: ### Key Sources\n40: \n41: - [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md): surface detection (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) and SKILLED language sub-detection\n42: - [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md) — Phase 1/2/3 lifecycle and phase-specific resource expectations\n43: - `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING — names the routing authority split and the compiled front door\n44: \n45: ---\n46: \n47: ## 2. INTENT MODEL\n48: \n49: Intent classification scores task text against weighted keyword signals to pick the dominant work type after surface detection. The vocabulary and weights are authored once, in the `INTENT_SIGNALS` block of §8 MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER; read them there. This section is the human-facing summary of how that block is scored, never a second copy of its keywords.\n50: \n51: ### Scoring algorithm\n52: \n53: 1. Sum weighted keyword hits from the request, target files, and known task context.\n54: 2. Boost explicit phase signals (verification, debugging, code quality) by +5 when the phase is unambiguous.\n55: 3. Select the top intent.\n56: 4. Select a second intent when the score delta to the next intent is small (`AMBIGUITY_DELTA = 1`).\n57: 5. For SKILLED surface, run language sub-detection after intent selection.\n58: \n59: ### Doc-only edit anti-signals\n60: \n61: Doc-only prose changes subtract from `sk-code` intent scoring and add to `sk-doc` scoring, even when the target file is under `.skilled/skills/`.\n62: \n63: | Prompt signal | Effect |\n64: | --- | --- |\n65: | `update headline`, `rewrite headline`, `headline section` | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n66: | `clarify description`, `clarify the X model`, `documentation section` | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n67: | `add a one-line summary`, `add summary at the top` | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n68: | `improve readme`, `reorganize readme`, `rewrite section` | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n69: | `SKILL.md` or `README.md` plus prose-only verbs | `sk-code -2`, `sk-doc +3` |\n70: \n71: Do not apply this anti-signal when the same request asks to modify executable code, parser logic, tests, shell behavior, TypeScript/Python entrypoints, JSON schema behavior, or routing algorithms. In that case, keep SKILLED surface detection and load `sk-code`.\n72: \n73: Multi-symptom prompts like `debug the broken jsonc descriptor` should load both DEBUGGING and CONFIG. Prompts like `update TypeScript advisor fixture` should load SKILLED plus TYPESCRIPT and CODE_QUALITY.\n74: \n75: ### Surface-specific intent notes\n76: \n77: - **SKILLED** intent scoring favors language standards, hooks, config, scripts, advisor/tests, metadata, and alignment verification.\n78: - Unsupported surfaces stay UNKNOWN even if intent scoring is strong.\n79: \n80: ---\n81: \n82: ## 3. LOAD TIERS\n83: \n84: | Tier | When | Resources |\n85: | --- | --- | --- |\n86: | ALWAYS | Every invocation | The §8 `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble only: `shared/references/stack-detection.md`, `shared/references/phase-detection.md` and `shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md`. The style guide `sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md` loads with CODE_QUALITY, and the shared doctrine loads with its intent: `workflow-debug.md` with DEBUGGING and `workflow-verify.md` with VERIFICATION |\n87: | SURFACE | After SKILLED detection | Surface-specific resources (`sk-code-skilled/references/shared/*`) |\n88: | INTENT | After intent classification | Implementation, debugging, verification, testing, etc. matching the top-1 intent (and top-2 when ambiguous) |\n89: | LANGUAGE | SKILLED only | Split JavaScript, TypeScript, Python, Shell, Rust, and Config standards from the matching `sk-code-skilled/references/<lang>/` resources |\n90: | ON_DEMAND | Explicit deep-dive keywords | Extended checklists and niche references (e.g. `sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md` for boundary templates) |\n91: \n92: ---\n93: \n94: ## 4. SKILLED MAP\n95: \n96: SKILLED loads from `sk-code-skilled/references/` and `sk-code-skilled/assets/`.\n97: \n98: ### Implementation shared tier\n99: \n100: IMPLEMENTATION loads the shared organization and naming baseline:\n101: \n102: - `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md`\n103: - `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md`\n104: \n105: ### Intent overlay\n106: \n107: | Intent | Resources |\n108: | --- | --- |\n109: | CODE_QUALITY | SKILLED only: `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md` plus the language-matching checklist, both when SKILLED is a resolved surface. A foreign surface loads the floor in `code-quality-standards.md` and its own declared rows |\n110: | VERIFICATION | `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/scripts/verify_alignment_drift.py` |\n111: | HOOKS | `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/hooks.md` |\n112: | CONFIG | `sk-code-skilled/references/config/*` |\n113: | LANGUAGE_STANDARDS | Detected language folder's `quick-reference.md`, `style-guide.md`, `quality-standards.md` |\n114: \n115: ### Language overlay\n116: \n117: | Language | Resources |\n118: | --- | --- |\n119: | JAVASCRIPT | `sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md` |\n120: | TYPESCRIPT | `sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md` |\n121: | PYTHON | `sk-code-skilled/references/python/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/python-checklist.md` |\n122: | SHELL | `sk-code-skilled/references/shell/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md` |\n123: | RUST | `sk-code-skilled/references/rust/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}/` (each split into topic parts), `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/` |\n124: | CONFIG | `sk-code-skilled/references/config/{style_guide,quality_standards,quick_reference}.md`, `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/config-checklist.md` |\n125: \n126: ### Authoring checklist gating\n127: \n128: When SKILLED intent is `authoring-new-X`, additionally load the matching authoring checklist:\n129: \n130: | Authoring target | Checklist |\n131: | --- | --- |\n132: | New skill | `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md` |\n133: | New agent | `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md` |\n134: | New command | `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/command-authoring.md` |\n135: | New MCP server | `sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md` |\n136: | Spec folder write | Now in system-spec-kit: `system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-authoring-checklist.md` + `system-spec-kit/references/workflows/spec-folder-write-recipe.md` |\n137: \n138: ### CI Workflow Map\n139: \n140: When the request names a GitHub Actions workflow, the hub bundles `sk-code-ci` from the request vocabulary and the `CI_WORKFLOW` intent loads its four rule references:\n141: \n142: | Resource | Carries |\n143: | --- | --- |\n144: | `sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md` | The rule index: path parity, SHA pins, README row, `set -euo pipefail`, fork and secret safety, matrix and `fail-fast` |\n145: | `sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md` | The twin rule, the rebuild-commit skip rule, push-only scope and the intentional exceptions table |\n146: | `sk-code-ci/references/job-shape-rules.md` | Timeouts, permissions, the concurrency template, the composite setup action and the switch `if:` shape |\n147: | `sk-code-ci/references/protected-set.md` | The repository CI profile and its keys, the workflows protected by function and the rules that follow |\n148: \n149: The map is the packet's own `RESOURCE_MAP` re-prefixed with `sk-code-ci/`, so the two stay equal.\n150: \n151: ### Frontend Map\n152: \n153: When a request asks about web frontend practice, or a Figma-to-code check, the hub bundles `sk-code-frontend` from request vocabulary alone:\n154: \n155: | Resource | Carries |\n156: | --- | --- |\n157: | `sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md` | The cross-stack core, token precedence, Figma boundary, and Frontend overrides contract |\n158: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md` | The web stack layer for markup, forms, layout, responsive behavior, media, and CSS architecture |\n159: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-elements-and-attributes.md` | Form autocomplete, dialog, and popover behavior |\n160: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-cascade-and-selectors.md` | Cascade layers and specificity conflicts |\n161: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-layout-and-sizing.md` | Container queries and stacking contexts |\n162: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-accessibility-patterns.md` | Accessibility patterns, visible focus, and live regions |\n163: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-performance-and-loading.md` | Web font loading and largest contentful paint |\n164: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-internationalization.md` | Right-to-left layout and bidirectional text |\n165: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-values.md` | Visual value decisions: spacing, type, weight, colour, shadow, radius, opacity, palettes and token sets |\n166: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-hierarchy.md` | Visual hierarchy and review: build procedure, symptom-to-fix tables, UX laws and the review pass |\n167: | `sk-code-frontend/references/web-motion-and-interaction.md` | Motion and interaction: animation, input, touch, focus, performance and feedback rules |\n168: | `sk-code-frontend/references/figma-to-code.md` | The five-pass check for comparing a built screen with its Figma frame |\n169: \n170: The map is the packet's own `RESOURCE_MAP` for the web, visual and Figma-to-code intents, re-prefixed with `sk-code-frontend/`, so the two stay equal there.\n171: \n172: ---\n173: \n174: ## 5. VERIFICATION COMMANDS\n175: \n176: | Surface | Required verification evidence |\n177: | --- | --- |\n178: | SKILLED | `python3 .skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-skilled/assets/scripts/verify_alignment_drift.py --root <changed-scope>`; plus targeted language/project tests such as vitest, pytest, shellcheck, JSON validation, or spec validation for changed spec folders |\n179: | UNKNOWN | User-selected verification command set before completion claim |\n180: \n181: ---\n182: \n183: ## 6. UNKNOWN FALLBACK\n184: \n185: If no supported surface matches, ask:\n186: \n187: 1. Is this `.skilled/` system code, or an unsupported stack?\n188: 2. Which files or directories are changing?\n189: 3. Which verification command proves the claim?\n190: 4. Should a new `sk-code` route be planned before implementation?\n191: \n192: Canonical sk-code owns the SKILLED surface, the `sk-code-ci` GitHub Actions surface and the stack-agnostic `sk-code-frontend` surface for web practice. Go, Next.js server code and React Native surfaces live in a project fork.\n193: \n194: ### UNKNOWN fallback checklist\n195: \n196: Returned when intent confidence is low (`max(intent_scores) < 0.5`) OR when the user explicitly asks for stack-agnostic guidance:\n197: \n198: - Confirm the detected surface (a declared specific surface, the SKILLED fallback or UNKNOWN)\n199: - Confirm the task intent (implementation / debugging / verification / etc.)\n200: - Provide one concrete input, error, or expected output\n201: - Confirm the verification command set before completion\n202: \n203: ---\n204: \n205: ## 7. LOADING DISCIPLINE (DO / DON'T)\n206: \n207: ### DO\n208: \n209: - Load only references needed for current intent + surface\n210: - Use exact canonical file paths in playbook result YAML (never directory placeholders)\n211: - Cite surface + top-1 intent + loaded resource paths + verification command in your final answer\n212: \n213: ### DON'T\n214: \n215: - Load all of `sk-code-skilled/` indiscriminately (\"just in case\")\n216: - Load Go, Next.js server code or React Native resources from canonical sk-code; those live in a project fork\n217: - Drop directory placeholders in playbook result YAML\n218: \n219: ---\n220: \n221: ## 8. MACHINE-READABLE ROUTER (replay / benchmark source)\n222: \n223: This block is the authored source for the intent vocabulary and weights. §2 explains how it is scored and does not repeat the keywords; §4 renders the same rows as prose. When an intent row changes here, update the matching prose row in §4.\n224: \n225: This projection is intentionally lossy in two documented ways the flat dictionary cannot express, both enforced by the prose contract and the surface-detection pseudocode in this file's §11:\n226: \n227: - **No phase boosts.** The `+5` unambiguous-phase boost from the §2 scoring algorithm is not represented; every keyword carries unit weight here.\n228: - **No doc-only anti-signals.** The `sk-code -2 / sk-doc +3` anti-signals (§2) that route prose-only edits away from this skill are a pre-filter the flat model does not carry.\n229: \n230: ```python\n231: # Always-loaded routing preamble: every code task detects stack, detects phase,\n232: # consults the router, and applies the universal quality baseline before any\n233: # surface-specific resource. These are loaded on every route.\n234: DEFAULT_RESOURCE = [\n235:     \"shared/references/stack-detection.md\",\n236:     \"shared/references/phase-detection.md\",\n237:     \"shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md\",\n238: ]\n239: \n240: INTENT_SIGNALS = {\n241:     \"IMPLEMENTATION\":     {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"implement\", \"build\", \"create\", \"feature\", \"component\", \"module\", \"authoring\"]},\n242:     \"CODE_QUALITY\":       {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"lint\", \"format\", \"quality gate\", \"naming\", \"standards\", \"code smell\", \"comment hygiene\", \"restraint\", \"yagni\", \"simplify\", \"over-engineering\", \"bloat\", \"skipped_untrusted\", \"untrusted repository check\", \"trustrepochecks\", \"quality config\", \"repository/quality.json\", \"configured check\", \"unverified\"]},\n243:     \"DEBUGGING\":          {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"debug\", \"broken\", \"failing\", \"stack trace\", \"console error\", \"regression\"]},\n244:     \"VERIFICATION\":       {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"verify\", \"passing\", \"type-check\", \"alignment drift\", \"completion claim\"]},\n245:     \"HOOKS\":              {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"session-prime\", \"user-prompt-submit\", \"pre-tool-use\", \"post-tool-use\"]},\n246:     \"CONFIG\":             {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"jsonc\", \".json\", \".jsonc\", \"descriptor\", \"config schema\"]},\n247:     \"JAVASCRIPT\":         {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"javascript\", \"vanilla js\", \".js\", \"commonjs\", \".cjs\", \".mjs\"]},\n248:     \"TYPESCRIPT\":         {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"typescript\", \".ts\", \".tsx\"]},\n249:     \"PYTHON\":             {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"python\", \".py\", \"docstring\"]},\n250:     \"SHELL\":              {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"shell script\", \"bash\", \".sh\"]},\n251:     \"RUST\":               {\"weight\": 1, \"keywords\": [\"rust\", \".rs\", \"cargo.toml\", \"cargo.lock\", \"napi-rs\", \"napi_rs\", \"#[napi]\", \"wasm-bindgen\", \"wasm_bindgen\", \"#[wasm_bindgen]\", \"wasi\", \"cdylib\"]},\n252:     \"CI_WORKFLOW\":        {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"github actions workflow\", \"ci workflow\", \"github actions yaml\", \"workflow path filters\", \"github workflow timeout\", \"github workflow concurrency\", \"github workflow permissions\", \"required status check workflow\", \"ci job\", \"action to a sha\", \"sha-pinned action\", \"github actions permissions\", \"ci workflows\", \"ci switch\", \"turn off ci\", \".github/workflows\", \"workflow concurrency\", \"github actions release workflow\", \"github actions deploy workflow\", \"release workflow yaml\", \"deploy workflow yaml\", \"github actions minutes\", \"actions minutes\", \"actions runner minutes\", \"runner minutes\", \"ci workflow cost\", \"ci cost\", \"reduce ci costs\", \"lower ci costs\", \"cut ci costs\", \"reduce our ci costs\", \"lower our ci costs\", \"ci spend\", \"ci bill\", \"ci minutes cost\", \"cut the ci minutes\", \"cut ci minutes\", \"reduce ci minutes\", \"ci pipeline cost\", \"ci pipeline cheaper\", \"ci pipelines cheaper\", \"cheaper ci pipeline\", \"ci runs cheaper\", \"failing ci\", \"failing ci pipeline\", \"ci failure\", \"ci failures\", \"ci is failing\", \"broken ci\", \"debug the ci\", \"debug ci\", \"cost of our ci\", \"make ci cheaper\", \"github actions bill\", \"github actions cost\", \"turn off the ci\", \"turn on the ci\", \"ci review workflow\"]},\n253:     \"FRONTEND_WEB\":        {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"semantic html markup\"]},\n254:     \"WEB_ELEMENTS\":        {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"html form autocomplete\", \"dialog element\", \"popover attribute\"]},\n255:     \"WEB_CASCADE\":         {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"css cascade layers\", \"css specificity conflict\"]},\n256:     \"WEB_LAYOUT\":          {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"container query layout\", \"css stacking context\"]},\n257:     \"WEB_A11Y\":            {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"web accessibility pattern\", \"focus visible styling\", \"keyboard focus\", \"broken tab order\", \"accessible name\", \"aria live region\", \"focus ring accessibility\", \"focus outline removed\", \"screen reader support\", \"aria label missing\", \"alt text missing\", \"wcag audit\"]},\n258:     \"WEB_PERF\":            {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"web font loading\", \"largest contentful paint\"]},\n259:     \"WEB_I18N\":            {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"right to left layout\", \"bidirectional text\"]},\n260:     \"WEB_VISUAL_VALUES\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"what padding should this have\", \"how much padding\", \"what font size should this be\", \"spacing between elements\", \"spacing scale\", \"type scale\", \"build a color palette\", \"shade ramp\", \"box shadow elevation\", \"border radius consistency\", \"contrast ratio failure\", \"grey text on colored background\", \"dark mode palette\", \"concentric border radius\", \"layered shadows\", \"shadow color on dark surface\", \"tabular numbers\", \"what should this button look like\", \"colour decision\", \"color decision\", \"padding\", \"type scale for\", \"font size\", \"colour palette\", \"color palette\", \"contrast ratio\", \"corner radius\", \"box shadow\", \"spacing between\", \"contrast of\", \"check the contrast\", \"colour contrast\", \"color contrast\", \"muted text\"]},\n261:     \"WEB_VISUAL_HIERARCHY\": {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"ui looks off\", \"looks amateur\", \"feels cluttered\", \"make this look better\", \"make this ui look designed\", \"visual hierarchy\", \"no visual hierarchy\", \"nothing draws the eye\", \"everything competes for attention\", \"everything looks the same\", \"emphasize by de-emphasizing\", \"how to style a destructive button\", \"this page feels overwhelming\", \"where do i start designing\", \"design a new screen from scratch\", \"how much white space\", \"why does this look wrong\", \"does this look right\", \"critique this design\", \"design critique\", \"visual audit\", \"design review findings\", \"design review\", \"review this design\", \"review this screen\", \"ux laws\", \"cognitive load\", \"progressive disclosure\", \"empty state design\", \"look wrong\", \"looks wrong\", \"looks off\", \"visual review\", \"type hierarchy\"]},\n262:     \"WEB_MOTION\":        {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"animation duration\", \"transition duration\", \"easing curve\", \"spring versus easing\", \"twelve principles of animation\", \"when not to animate\", \"response time budget\", \"touch target size\", \"hover state\", \"active pressed state\", \"disabled state styling\", \"button states\", \"animation\", \"transition timing\", \"easing\", \"hover\", \"focus-visible\", \"interaction states\"]},\n263:     \"FIGMA_TO_CODE\":      {\"weight\": 4, \"keywords\": [\"figma to code\", \"check built screen against figma\", \"compare build with figma frame\", \"figma frame\", \"figma frame check\", \"implement a figma design\"]},\n264: }\n265: \n266: RESOURCE_MAP = {\n267:     \"IMPLEMENTATION\": [\n268:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/naming-and-commenting.md\",\n269:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/universal-patterns/organization-security-and-examples.md\",\n270:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/code-organization/overview-and-module-organization.md\",\n271:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/code-organization/imports-and-exports.md\",\n272:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/code-organization/directory-and-test-conventions.md\",\n273:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/agent-authoring.md\",\n274:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/command-authoring.md\",\n275:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/skill-authoring.md\",\n276:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/mcp-server-authoring.md\",\n277: \n278:     ],\n279:     \"CODE_QUALITY\": [\n280:         \"shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md\",\n281:         \"shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md\",\n282:         \"sk-code-quality/references/code-style-guide.md\",\n283:         \"sk-code-quality/references/runner-contract.md\",\n284:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/universal-checklist.md\",  # SKILLED only: the house checklist loads when SKILLED resolves, never for a foreign surface\n285:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/javascript-checklist.md\",\n286:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/js-css-style/overview-header-and-comments.md\",\n287:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/js-css-style/naming-init-formatting-and-css.md\",\n288:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/js-css-style/verification-quick-reference-and-related.md\",\n289:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/typescript-checklist.md\",\n290:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/python-checklist.md\",\n291:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/shell-checklist.md\",\n292:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/overview-and-p0-parity.md\",\n293:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p0-safety-and-boundary-discipline.md\",\n294:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p1-required.md\",\n295:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/rust-checklist/p2-evidence-validation-and-resources.md\",\n296:         \"sk-code-review/assets/code-quality-checklist.md\",\n297: \n298:     ],\n299:     \"DEBUGGING\": [\n300:         \"shared/references/workflow-debug.md\",\n301: \n302:     ],\n303:     \"VERIFICATION\": [\n304:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/alignment-verification-automation.md\",\n305:         \"shared/references/workflow-verify.md\",\n306:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/scripts/README.md\",\n307: \n308:     ],\n309:     \"HOOKS\": [\n310:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shared/hooks.md\",\n311:     ],\n312:     \"CONFIG\": [\n313:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/config/style-guide.md\",\n314:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/config/quality-standards.md\",\n315:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/config/quick-reference.md\",\n316:         \"sk-code-skilled/assets/checklists/config-checklist.md\",\n317:     ],\n318:     \"JAVASCRIPT\": [\n319:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/style-guide.md\",\n320:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quality-standards/overview-modules-and-docs.md\",\n321:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quality-standards/security-testing-and-exemptions.md\",\n322:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/javascript/quick-reference.md\",\n323:     ],\n324:     \"TYPESCRIPT\": [\n325:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/style-guide/overview-strict-and-naming.md\",\n326:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/style-guide/formatting-imports-and-coexistence.md\",\n327:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quality-standards/overview-and-type-system.md\",\n328:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quality-standards/tsdoc-errors-and-async.md\",\n329:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quality-standards/tsconfig-and-modules.md\",\n330:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quick-reference/template-naming-and-types.md\",\n331:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/quick-reference/imports-errors-and-tsconfig.md\",\n332:     ],\n333:     \"PYTHON\": [\n334:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/python/style-guide.md\",\n335:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/python/quality-standards.md\",\n336:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/python/quick-reference.md\",\n337:     ],\n338:     \"SHELL\": [\n339:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/style-guide/overview-structure-and-naming.md\",\n340:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/style-guide/variables-functions-and-output.md\",\n341:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/quality-standards/overview-and-priority-blockers.md\",\n342:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/quality-standards/validation-security-and-shellcheck.md\",\n343:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/quick-reference/template-variables-and-loops.md\",\n344:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/shell/quick-reference/functions-strings-and-checklist.md\",\n345:     ],\n346:     \"RUST\": [\n347:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/overview-and-file-header.md\",\n348:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/toolchain-and-project-structure.md\",\n349:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/naming-conventions.md\",\n350:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/formatting-and-imports.md\",\n351:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/commenting-and-rustdoc.md\",\n352:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/interop-model.md\",\n353:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/style-guide/interop-errors-and-parity.md\",\n354:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/overview-and-data-ownership.md\",\n355:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/modeling-collections-and-api.md\",\n356:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/docs-errors-and-async.md\",\n357:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/build-and-organization.md\",\n358:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quality-standards/determinism-and-parity.md\",\n359:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/overview-and-boundary-template.md\",\n360:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/naming-ordering-and-signatures.md\",\n361:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/collections-imports-and-errors.md\",\n362:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/rustdoc-and-cargo.md\",\n363:         \"sk-code-skilled/references/rust/quick-reference/determinism-parity-and-related.md\",\n364:     ],\n365:     \"CI_WORKFLOW\": [\n366:         \"sk-code-ci/references/ci-workflow-standards.md\",\n367:         \"sk-code-ci/references/trigger-and-filter-rules.md\",\n368:         \"sk-code-ci/references/job-shape-rules.md\",\n369:         \"sk-code-ci/references/protected-set.md\",\n370:     ],\n371:     \"FRONTEND_WEB\": [\n372:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n373:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n374:     ],\n375:     \"WEB_ELEMENTS\": [\n376:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n377:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n378:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-elements-and-attributes.md\",\n379:     ],\n380:     \"WEB_CASCADE\": [\n381:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n382:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n383:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-cascade-and-selectors.md\",\n384:     ],\n385:     \"WEB_LAYOUT\": [\n386:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n387:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n388:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-layout-and-sizing.md\",\n389:     ],\n390:     \"WEB_A11Y\": [\n391:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n392:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n393:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-accessibility-patterns.md\",\n394:     ],\n395:     \"WEB_PERF\": [\n396:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n397:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n398:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-performance-and-loading.md\",\n399:     ],\n400:     \"WEB_I18N\": [\n401:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n402:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-markup-and-css.md\",\n403:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-internationalization.md\",\n404:     ],\n405:     \"WEB_VISUAL_VALUES\": [\n406:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n407:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-values.md\",\n408:     ],\n409:     \"WEB_VISUAL_HIERARCHY\": [\n410:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n411:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-hierarchy.md\",\n412:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-visual-values.md\",\n413:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-accessibility-patterns.md\",\n414:     ],\n415:     \"WEB_MOTION\": [\n416:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n417:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/web-motion-and-interaction.md\",\n418:     ],\n419:     \"FIGMA_TO_CODE\": [\n420:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/figma-to-code.md\",\n421:         \"sk-code-frontend/references/frontend-practice.md\",\n422:     ],\n423: }\n424: \n425: # Hub-level shared controls, declared once here. This list holds every\n426: # contained shared/ path that RESOURCE_MAP references but that is exempt from\n427: # typed-leaf projection, because it has no single packet owner. The root-router\n428: # contract validates each entry and requires RESOURCE_MAP to reference it.\n429: # DEFAULT_RESOURCE above is the other half of the set: its always-loaded\n430: # preamble paths are hub-level controls too. No other shared/ path is a\n431: # control, so a guard that needs the control set reads these two lists.\n432: SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES = [\n433:     \"shared/references/universal/code-quality-standards.md\",\n434:     \"shared/references/universal/rule-overrides.md\",\n435:     \"shared/references/workflow-debug.md\",\n436:     \"shared/references/workflow-verify.md\",\n437: ]\n438: ```\n439: \n440: ### Surface-aware loading (route-time)\n441: \n442: The router does NOT load the whole matched-intent union. After surface detection (§1, `stack-detection.md`), a route loads:\n443: \n444: - the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble (stack/phase detection and the universal quality baseline), plus\n445: - the `shared/` entries the matched intents map (for example `code-quality-standards.md` under CODE_QUALITY, `workflow-debug.md` under DEBUGGING and `workflow-verify.md` under VERIFICATION), never the whole `shared/references/universal/` folder, plus\n446: - only the **detected surface's** slice (`<surface>/references/*`) for the matched intents.\n447: \n448: - It does not load another surface's resources.\n449: - It defers `assets/*` (checklists, recipes, templates) to on-demand rather than the first slice.\n450: - Within SKILLED it slices once more by the **detected language** (§1 sub-detection): a TypeScript task loads `sk-code-skilled/references/typescript/*` plus the language-agnostic `sk-code-skilled/references/shared/*`, not the Python, shell, config, or JavaScript folders.\n451: - An `UNKNOWN` surface falls back to the preamble plus the shared-tier entries its matched intents map.\n452: \n453: This is what stops a routine single-surface task from pulling the full cross-surface set. The deterministic router-replay enforces the same rule, so the benchmark measures it.\n454: \n455: ---\n456: \n457: ## 9. HOW TO READ THIS\n458: \n459: - Every code task first loads the always-on preamble (stack + phase detection and the universal quality baseline); routing then narrows to the detected surface's slice for the matched intents.\n460: - One dominant intent on one surface loads that surface's slice; two near-tied intents (within the router's ambiguity delta) load both intent sets, deduped by canonical pair.\n461: - A single-surface task never pulls another surface's resources.\n462: - No keyword match, or an `UNKNOWN` surface, falls back to the preamble plus the shared-tier entries its matched intents map — confirm the surface and intent before loading more. See §6 (UNKNOWN FALLBACK) and §7 (LOADING DISCIPLINE) for the full rules.\n463: \n464: ---\n465: \n466: ## 10. RELATED RESOURCES\n467: \n468: - [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md): surface detection (a declared specific surface, then the SKILLED fallback, then UNKNOWN) + SKILLED language sub-detection\n469: - [`phase-detection.md`](shared/references/phase-detection.md) — Phase 1/2/3 lifecycle and per-phase resource loading\n470: - `SKILL.md` §2 SMART ROUTING — names the routing authority split and the compiled front door\n471: - Barter equivalent: `barter/.skilled/skills/sk-code/shared/references/smart-routing.md` (different routing key — git-remote project — but same structural pattern)\n472: \n473: ---\n474: \n475: ## 11. MACHINE ROUTER\n476: \n477: The hub's detailed routing prose, moved here from `SKILL.md` so the skill body keeps only a short routing summary and a pointer to this file. It sits last, after the stage-two map above, and it declares no map of its own: the `INTENT_SIGNALS` and `RESOURCE_MAP` block in section 8 stays the only replay source.\n478: \n479: > **Compiled routing (default-on, flag-gated, additive).** Resolve the mode via the compiled router contract first:\n480: > ```bash\n481: > node .skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs --hub sk-code --prompt \"<task>\"\n482: > ```\n483: > Follow the returned decision: `route` (use its `targets`), `clarify`/`defer` (disambiguate) or `reject` (refuse). On a `{\"servingAuthority\":\"legacy\"}` sentinel or any error, use the routing below. The front door self-gates on serving-authority. Compiled routing is now the default for `sk-code`. Set `SPECKIT_COMPILED_ROUTING=0`, the explicit kill-switch, to force legacy routing fleet-wide.\n484: \n485: **Session surface hint.** Before you call the front door, run the surface detection in [`stack-detection.md`](shared/references/stack-detection.md) on the session's working directory and the files it is editing.\n486: Then add its result as `--surface-hint <SURFACE>`, for example `--surface-hint SKILLED`.\n487: When the prompt's keywords tie between surfaces, a hinted specific surface leads them.\n488: Workflow modes keep first place, and the targets and the bundle kind stay the same.\n489: `SKILLED` is the fallback surface and always ranks last, so a `SKILLED` hint changes nothing.\n490: With `UNKNOWN`, no hint or a surface the prompt did not match, the `routerPolicy.tieBreak` order applies.\n491: \n492: ### Surface Router: per-intent leaf sets\n493: \n494: Root `ROUTER.md` owns the second-stage `INTENT_SIGNALS` / `RESOURCE_MAP`, while packet-owned resources stay typed through `leaf-manifest.json`.\n495: The hub-level shared controls are declared once, in `ROUTER.md` §8: the `SHARED_CONTROL_RESOURCES` list plus the always-loaded `DEFAULT_RESOURCE` preamble. They resolve on disk but never project as leaves.\n496: The hub router keeps stage-one mode selection and its `shared/README.md` fallback.\n497: \n498: ### The discriminator\n499: - **`workflowMode`** - the public mode/packet key: `sk-code-quality`, `sk-code-review` (workflow) or `sk-code-ci`, `sk-code-frontend`, `sk-code-skilled` (surface).\n500: - **`packetKind`** - the axis: `workflow` (a mode that acts) or `surface` (read-only evidence bundled alongside a mode).\n501: - **`backendKind`** - which backend runs the packet: `surface-router` or `review-cache` for workflow modes, `evidence-base` for surface packets.\n502: \n503: Surface packets are advisor-invisible (`routingClass: metadata`, read-only `toolSurface`).\n504: The advisor still routes the single identity `sk-code`, and the hub bundles zero-or-more surfaces as evidence via `routerPolicy.outcomes.surfaceBundle`.\n505: The workflow mode comes first, followed by surfaces.\n506: \"review my typescript module for jank\" → `[sk-code-review, sk-code-skilled]`.\n507: Bundled surfaces follow the `routerPolicy.tieBreak` order. It ranks `sk-code-frontend` ahead of `sk-code-ci`, then `sk-code-skilled` last, because a new surface goes ahead of the existing ones and the SKILLED fallback stays last.\n508: \n509: ### Surface detection\n510: \n511: Surface detection lives once in the hub's `shared/` layer and every mode and surface consumes it.\n512: It tries every declared specific surface first, then the `SKILLED` fallback, then `UNKNOWN`.\n513: When a specific surface and `SKILLED` both match, the specific one leads and `SKILLED` comes along as second evidence.\n514: Each surface declares its markers in a `surfaceDetection` block in `mode-registry.json`, or in its own `graph-metadata.json` when it lives outside the hub.\n515: Modes own workflow contracts, not surface identity.\n516: `sk-code-ci` and `sk-code-frontend` are the surfaces that stack detection does not select. The hub bundles both from request vocabulary alone: CI through `code-ci-aliases` and `code-ci-runtime`, and Frontend through `code-frontend-aliases` in `hub-router.json`.\n517: \n518: ### Fallback route\n519: \n520: When no workflow mode dominates (a bare implement/debug/verify request), the router defers to a pure surface bundle: it detects the surface, loads that surface's evidence and workflow doctrine, and the agent acts. `routerPolicy.defaultMode` is `null`, so the hub does not force a stale default. An unclear code intent asks for disambiguation.\n521: \n522: Per-mode behavior is **not flattened**: each packet keeps its own code-work contract, standards, evidence rules, and tool-permission guards.\n523: \n524: ### Routing rule\n525: ```python\n526: from pathlib import Path\n527: \n528: SKILL_ROOT = Path(__file__).resolve().parent\n529: UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST = [\n530:     \"Confirm whether this is sk-code-quality, sk-code-review, sk-code-ci, sk-code-frontend or sk-code-skilled work\",\n531:     \"Confirm the target files or runtime surface\",\n532:     \"Confirm the expected action: implement, debug, verify, quality gate, or review\",\n533:     \"Confirm the verification command set before completion\",\n534: ]\n535: \n536: def _guard_in_skill(relative_path: str) -> str:\n537:     resolved = (SKILL_ROOT / relative_path).resolve()\n538:     resolved.relative_to(SKILL_ROOT)\n539:     if resolved.name != \"SKILL.md\" and resolved.suffix.lower() != \".md\":\n540:         raise ValueError(f\"Only markdown skill resources are routable: {relative_path}\")\n541:     return resolved.relative_to(SKILL_ROOT).as_posix()\n542: \n543: def load_if_available(relative_path: str, loaded: list[str], seen: set[str]) -> None:\n544:     guarded = _guard_in_skill(relative_path)\n545:     path = SKILL_ROOT / guarded\n546:     if path.exists() and guarded not in seen:\n547:         load(guarded)\n548:         loaded.append(guarded)\n549:         seen.add(guarded)\n550: \n551: def route(task):\n552:     loaded, seen = [], set()\n553:     registry = read_json(SKILL_ROOT / \"mode-registry.json\")\n554:     workflow_mode = explicit_mode_hint(task) or classify_workflow_mode(task)\n555: \n556:     if not workflow_mode:\n557:         load_if_available(\"shared/README.md\", loaded, seen)\n558:         return {\n559:             \"load_level\": \"UNKNOWN_FALLBACK\",\n560:             \"needs_disambiguation\": True,\n561:             \"disambiguation_checklist\": UNKNOWN_FALLBACK_CHECKLIST,\n562:             \"resources\": loaded,\n563:         }\n564: \n565:     entry = find_registry_entry(registry, workflowMode=workflow_mode)\n566:     if not entry:\n567:         load_if_available(\"shared/README.md\", loaded, seen)\n568:         return {\"load_level\": \"UNKNOWN_FALLBACK\", \"unknown_mode\": workflow_mode, \"resources\": loaded}\n569: \n570:     packet_skill = f\"{entry['packet']}/SKILL.md\"\n571:     load_if_available(packet_skill, loaded, seen)\n572: \n573:     for surface_mode in surface_bundle_from_hub_router(task):\n574:         surface_entry = find_registry_entry(registry, workflowMode=surface_mode)\n575:         if surface_entry:\n576:             load_if_available(f\"{surface_entry['packet']}/SKILL.md\", loaded, seen)\n577: \n578:     if not loaded:\n579:         load_if_available(\"shared/README.md\", loaded, seen)\n580:         return {\"load_level\": \"UNKNOWN_FALLBACK\", \"notice\": \"matched registry but no packet SKILL.md was available\", \"resources\": loaded}\n581: \n582:     return {\"workflowMode\": workflow_mode, \"backendKind\": entry[\"backendKind\"], \"resources\": loaded}\n583: ```"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.opencode/skills/sk-code/ROUTER.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794522575411,"end":1794522576336}}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794522576358,"sessionID":"ses_e2e209366ffe3qZDn1hMKZPNUG","part":{"partID":"prt_1d1e173bb001gqbQi2mzXX69Nj","sessionID":"ses_e2e209366ffe3qZDn1hMKZPNUG","messageID":"msg_1d1e15b05001m11c56AZQYgBlH","type":"tool","id":"call_aa55c35c8ce342db8ea3ea87","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs, lines 1-644\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: COMMAND ROUTING BASELINE CAPTURE\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // Captures how the built scorer routes the command-prompt corpus under the\n6: // reproducible filesystem projection (empty SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DB_DIR →\n7: // SQLite loader returns null → projection built purely from committed graph\n8: // metadata). It answers the questions command surfacing has to hold to: a\n9: // prompt that names a slash command reaches that command's bridge, a prompt\n10: // that must stay quiet offers no command at all, a partial slash mention is\n11: // not routed as if it were complete, and a bridge ranked below the top skill\n12: // is owned by that skill.\n13: //\n14: // It also measures what the compiled route adds on top of that: a hub can name\n15: // the slash command its own routing would take, which reaches a reader who never\n16: // types a slash command. That reach is reported under its own key so every\n17: // existing number keeps the value and the comparability it had before.\n18: //\n19: // The result is the ratchet baseline: the gate re-scores live under the same\n20: // environment and holds every count to it, so any scoring or bridge-metadata\n21: // change that moves a number forces a conscious re-capture rather than\n22: // drifting silently. `corpusSha256` pins the baseline to the exact corpus it\n23: // was captured against, so a corpus edit changes the hash and forces\n24: // re-baselining.\n25: //\n26: // The same corpus can also be scored against the operator's own skill graph and\n27: // embedder under `--real-embedding`, which is a live probe of this machine rather\n28: // than a reproducible projection. Its numbers describe an environment, so it never\n29: // writes the baseline and never runs under the fixture regime the ratchet replays.\n30: //\n31: // Setup runs on call, never on import: the environment projection, the clock\n32: // pin and the scorer import all live inside scoreCommandCorpus(), so a harness\n33: // may import this module and score in-process without inheriting either.\n34: //\n35: // Usage:\n36: //   node capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs            # print JSON to stdout\n37: //   node capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs --write    # also write the baseline\n38: //   node capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs --real-embedding   # score live vectors\n39: \n40: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n41: // 1. IMPORTS\n42: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n43: \n44: import { execFileSync, spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\n45: import { createHash } from 'node:crypto';\n46: import { existsSync, mkdtempSync, readdirSync, readFileSync, rmSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\n47: import { createRequire } from 'node:module';\n48: import { tmpdir } from 'node:os';\n49: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n50: import { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';\n51: import { rejectUnknownOptions } from './reject-unknown-options.mjs';\n52: \n53: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n54: // 2. CONSTANTS\n55: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n56: \n57: const HERE = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n58: const CORPUS_JSONL = resolve(HERE, 'command-prompts.jsonl');\n59: const BRIDGES_JSON = resolve(HERE, '../command-bridges/command-bridges.generated.json');\n60: const SCORER_EVAL_BASELINE_JSON = resolve(HERE, 'scorer-eval-baseline.json');\n61: const OUTPUT_JSON = resolve(HERE, 'command-routing-baseline.json');\n62: const DIST = resolve(HERE, '../../dist/runtime');\n63: const FUSION_MODULE = resolve(DIST, 'lib/scorer/fusion.js');\n64: const WORKSPACE_ROOT_MODULE = resolve(DIST, 'lib/utils/workspace-root.js');\n65: const COMMAND_ATTACH_MODULE = resolve(DIST, 'lib/command-attach.js');\n66: const COMPILED_ROUTING_FLAG_MODULE = resolve(DIST, 'lib/compiled-routing-flag.js');\n67: const SEMANTIC_SHADOW_MODULE = resolve(DIST, 'lib/scorer/lanes/semantic-shadow.js');\n68: const SKILL_GRAPH_DB_MODULE = resolve(DIST, 'lib/skill-graph/skill-graph-db.js');\n69: const DIST_FRESHNESS_MODULE = resolve(HERE, '../../../../system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/lib/dist-freshness.cjs');\n70: \n71: const SENTINEL = '.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md';\n72: const COMMANDS_DIR = '.skilled/commands';\n73: // Only the folder-level README is a document; a lowercase `readme.md` shipped\n74: // beside the commands is itself a command, so the skip is case-sensitive.\n75: const COMMAND_DOC_SKIP = 'README.md';\n76: const COMMAND_DOC_SUFFIX = '.md';\n77: // The compiled route is asked for through the public front door a hub's SKILL.md\n78: // calls, so this measures the decision the runtime actually serves rather than a\n79: // second derivation of it that could disagree with the first.\n80: const COMPILED_ROUTE_CLI = '.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs';\n81: const COMPILED_ROUTE_TIMEOUT_MS = 5000;\n82: \n83: // A real capture talks to the same embedder the recommend path does, so the probe\n84: // asks that daemon directly and names the model family the routing vectors come\n85: // from. The command vectors sit beside the skill vectors in the skill graph, in a\n86: // table whose name carries the embedder dimension.\n87: const EMBEDDER_TAGS_URL = 'http://localhost:11434/api/tags';\n88: const EMBEDDER_MODEL_PREFIX = 'nomic-embed-text';\n89: const EMBEDDER_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS = 5000;\n90: const COMMAND_VECTOR_TABLE_PREFIX = 'vec_cmd_';\n91: \n92: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n93: // 3. RUNTIME SETUP\n94: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n95: //\n96: // Only the value lookups live here; the mutations themselves are deferred into\n97: // scoreCommandCorpus() so importing this module has no side effects.\n98: \n99: function isCalendarDate(value) {\n100:   if (!/^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$/.test(value)) return false;\n101:   const parsed = new Date(`${value}T00:00:00Z`);\n102:   return !Number.isNaN(parsed.getTime()) && parsed.toISOString().slice(0, 10) === value;\n103: }\n104: \n105: // The derived lane halves each skill's score every 90 days of age, measured\n106: // from its derived.generated_at to the scoring clock (floored at 0.1), so\n107: // scores move with the calendar. The scoring date is therefore inherited from\n108: // the scorer-eval baseline and pinned, never read from the wall clock: a\n109: // recapture on any day replays the recorded date and reproduces the same\n110: // numbers, which keeps the two baselines directly comparable.\n111: function readPinnedScoringDate() {\n112:   let recorded;\n113:   try {\n114:     recorded = JSON.parse(readFileSync(SCORER_EVAL_BASELINE_JSON, 'utf8')).capturedAt;\n115:   } catch {\n116:     recorded = undefined;\n117:   }\n118:   if (typeof recorded !== 'string' || !isCalendarDate(recorded)) {\n119:     throw new Error(`scorer-eval baseline has no pinned scoring date (capturedAt): ${SCORER_EVAL_BASELINE_JSON}`);\n120:   }\n121:   return recorded;\n122: }\n123: \n124: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n125: // 4. REAL EMBEDDING PREFLIGHT\n126: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n127: //\n128: // A real capture scores whatever this machine's cosine lane can see, so both\n129: // halves of that lane are proved before a single row is scored: an embedder that\n130: // answers and a populated command vector table. Each half is named as itself when\n131: // it is missing, because a silent cosine lane reads exactly like a scoring change.\n132: \n133: async function assertEmbedderAnswers() {\n134:   const listed = [];\n135:   try {\n136:     const response = await fetch(EMBEDDER_TAGS_URL, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(EMBEDDER_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS) });\n137:     if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);\n138:     const payload = await response.json();\n139:     for (const model of Array.isArray(payload?.models) ? payload.models : []) {\n140:       if (typeof model?.name === 'string') listed.push(model.name);\n141:     }\n142:   } catch (error) {\n143:     const message = error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error);\n144:     throw new Error(`real embedding needs a live embedder: GET ${EMBEDDER_TAGS_URL} failed (${message})`);\n145:   }\n146:   // The stored vectors were built with this model family, so a daemon serving some\n147:   // other model cannot answer them no matter how well the dimensions line up.\n148:   if (!listed.some((name) => name.startsWith(EMBEDDER_MODEL_PREFIX))) {\n149:     throw new Error(`real embedding needs an embedder serving ${EMBEDDER_MODEL_PREFIX}*: GET ${EMBEDDER_TAGS_URL} lists ${listed.length > 0 ? listed.join(', ') : 'no models'}`);\n150:   }\n151: }\n152: \n153: function assertCommandVectorsPresent(database, databasePath) {\n154:   if (!database) {\n155:     throw new Error(`real embedding needs command vectors: the skill graph database is absent (${databasePath})`);\n156:   }\n157:   // GLOB keeps the trailing underscore of the prefix literal, so the match is the\n158:   // `vec_cmd_<dim>` family alone rather than any name shaped like it.\n159:   const tables = database\n160:     .prepare(\"SELECT name FROM sqlite_master WHERE type = 'table' AND name GLOB ?\")\n161:     .all(`${COMMAND_VECTOR_TABLE_PREFIX}*`)\n162:     .map((table) => table.name);\n163:   if (tables.length === 0) {\n164:     throw new Error(`real embedding needs command vectors: no ${COMMAND_VECTOR_TABLE_PREFIX}* table in ${databasePath}`);\n165:   }\n166:   const populated = tables.filter((name) => database.prepare(`SELECT COUNT(*) AS rowCount FROM \"${name}\"`).get().rowCount > 0);\n167:   if (populated.length === 0) {\n168:     throw new Error(`real embedding needs command vectors: ${tables.join(', ')} has no rows in ${databasePath}`);\n169:   }\n170: }\n171: \n172: // The wrapper, the embedder and the command vectors are resolved together because\n173: // a real capture needs all three or it measures nothing worth reporting.\n174: async function prepareRealEmbeddingScoring() {\n175:   if (!existsSync(SEMANTIC_SHADOW_MODULE)) {\n176:     throw new Error(`built semantic-shadow file missing: ${SEMANTIC_SHADOW_MODULE}`);\n177:   }\n178:   const { withSemanticShadowPromptEmbedding } = await import(pathToFileURL(SEMANTIC_SHADOW_MODULE).href);\n179:   const { getDbReadOnly, getSkillGraphDbPath } = await import(pathToFileURL(SKILL_GRAPH_DB_MODULE).href);\n180:   await assertEmbedderAnswers();\n181:   assertCommandVectorsPresent(getDbReadOnly(), getSkillGraphDbPath());\n182:   return withSemanticShadowPromptEmbedding;\n183: }\n184: \n185: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n186: // 5. HELPERS\n187: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n188: \n189: function sha256File(path) {\n190:   return `sha256:${createHash('sha256').update(readFileSync(path)).digest('hex')}`;\n191: }\n192: \n193: // Line numbers are reported as the physical line a bad row sits on, so a\n194: // parse failure points at the line to repair rather than at a row ordinal.\n195: function readCorpus(path) {\n196:   if (!existsSync(path)) throw new Error(`corpus file missing: ${path}`);\n197:   const lines = readFileSync(path, 'utf8').split('\\n');\n198:   const rows = [];\n199:   for (let index = 0; index < lines.length; index += 1) {\n200:     const line = lines[index].trim();\n201:     if (line === '') continue;\n202:     try {\n203:       rows.push(JSON.parse(line));\n204:     } catch (error) {\n205:       throw new Error(`invalid JSON on corpus line ${index + 1} of ${path}: ${error.message}`);\n206:     }\n207:   }\n208:   if (rows.length === 0) throw new Error(`corpus file empty: ${path}`);\n209:   return rows;\n210: }\n211: \n212: // Commands live exactly two levels deep, `<namespace>/<name>.md`, and become\n213: // `/<namespace>:<name>`. Anything directly in the commands root is a document\n214: // rather than a command, and deeper nesting is a command's own assets.\n215: function readCommandFileNames(workspaceRoot) {\n216:   const commandsRoot = resolve(workspaceRoot, COMMANDS_DIR);\n217:   const commands = [];\n218:   for (const namespace of readdirSync(commandsRoot, { withFileTypes: true })) {\n219:     if (!namespace.isDirectory()) continue;\n220:     for (const file of readdirSync(join(commandsRoot, namespace.name), { withFileTypes: true })) {\n221:       if (!file.isFile()) continue;\n222:       if (!file.name.endsWith(COMMAND_DOC_SUFFIX) || file.name === COMMAND_DOC_SKIP) continue;\n223:       commands.push(`/${namespace.name}:${file.name.slice(0, -COMMAND_DOC_SUFFIX.length)}`);\n224:     }\n225:   }\n226:   return commands.sort();\n227: }\n228: \n229: // The TypeScript inventory id is what the scorer actually emits, and it differs\n230: // from the human command name, so the bridge id is the map key. A command with\n231: // only the Python runtime enabled has no TypeScript id to emit and is reported\n232: // as unreachable instead.\n233: function readBridgeMap(path) {\n234:   const bridges = JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'));\n235:   const idToCommand = new Map();\n236:   for (const entry of bridges.entries ?? []) {\n237:     const typescript = entry.runtime?.typescript;\n238:     if (typescript?.enabled !== true || typeof typescript.id !== 'string') continue;\n239:     idToCommand.set(typescript.id, { command: entry.command, skillId: entry.skillId });\n240:   }\n241:   return { bridgeCounts: bridges.counts ?? {}, idToCommand };\n242: }\n243: \n244: function commandOf(idToCommand, id) {\n245:   return idToCommand.get(id)?.command ?? null;\n246: }\n247: \n248: // The route arrives from a separate process, so a spawn that fails, times out or\n249: // prints anything unparsable yields no route: a broken read must degrade to \"no\n250: // command offered\" rather than to an invented one.\n251: function readCompiledRoute(workspaceRoot, hubId, prompt) {\n252:   try {\n253:     const stdout = execFileSync('node', [join(workspaceRoot, COMPILED_ROUTE_CLI), '--hub', hubId, '--prompt-stdin'], {\n254:       cwd: workspaceRoot,\n255:       input: prompt,\n256:       encoding: 'utf8',\n257:       timeout: COMPILED_ROUTE_TIMEOUT_MS,\n258:       stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'ignore'],\n259:     });\n260:     const route = JSON.parse(stdout);\n261:     return typeof route === 'object' && route !== null ? route : undefined;\n262:   } catch {\n263:     return undefined;\n264:   }\n265: }\n266: \n267: // A bridge that outranks nothing still stays reachable: when the two leaders are\n268: // a bridge and the skill that owns it, the reader gets the command without the\n269: // bridge displacing the skill the prompt actually asked for. The order is\n270: // irrelevant because the scorer's tie-break says nothing about ownership.\n271: function isBridgeOwnerPair(idToCommand, ids, expected) {\n272:   const [first, second] = ids;\n273:   if (first === undefined || second === undefined) return false;\n274:   const leading = idToCommand.get(first);\n275:   if (leading && leading.command === expected && leading.skillId === second) return true;\n276:   const trailing = idToCommand.get(second);\n277:   return Boolean(trailing && trailing.command === expected && trailing.skillId === first);\n278: }\n279: \n280: // The scorer reports ranked recommendations when the projection produced a\n281: // list, and only a top pick otherwise; both shapes have to be scorable.\n282: function recommendationIds(result) {\n283:   if (Array.isArray(result?.recommendations)) {\n284:     return result.recommendations.slice(0, 3).map((recommendation) => recommendation.skill);\n285:   }\n286:   return result?.topSkill === null || result?.topSkill === undefined ? [] : [result.topSkill];\n287: }\n288: \n289: function emptyTally() {\n290:   return { rows: 0, top1: 0, top3: 0 };\n291: }\n292: \n293: function addRow(tally, top1Hit, top3Hit) {\n294:   tally.rows += 1;\n295:   if (top1Hit) tally.top1 += 1;\n296:   if (top3Hit) tally.top3 += 1;\n297: }\n298: \n299: // Attached reach is kept apart from bridge reach and only ever counts a row the\n300: // ranked bridge did not already land, so `own` and `attached` partition the rows\n301: // and their sum is real reach rather than one row claimed twice.\n302: function emptyAttachedTally() {\n303:   return { own: 0, attached: 0, total: 0 };\n304: }\n305: \n306: function addAttachedRow(tally, ownHit, attachedHit) {\n307:   tally.total += 1;\n308:   if (ownHit) tally.own += 1;\n309:   if (attachedHit) tally.attached += 1;\n310: }\n311: \n312: function attachedTallyJson(tally) {\n313:   return { own: tally.own, attached: tally.attached, combined: tally.own + tally.attached, total: tally.total };\n314: }\n315: \n316: // Commands are named `<namespace>:<name>`, so the namespace is the workflow\n317: // family a reach number belongs to.\n318: function commandNamespace(command) {\n319:   if (typeof command !== 'string' || !command.startsWith('/')) return null;\n320:   const colon = command.indexOf(':');\n321:   return colon > 1 ? command.slice(1, colon) : null;\n322: }\n323: \n324: // Individual rows roll up to a family only when their command names one, so a\n325: // command outside the `<namespace>:<name>` grammar still counts in the split\n326: // total without inventing a family for itself.\n327: function attachedFamilyTally(byFamily, command) {\n328:   const namespace = commandNamespace(command);\n329:   if (namespace === null) return undefined;\n330:   if (!byFamily.has(namespace)) {\n331:     byFamily.set(namespace, { tune: emptyAttachedTally(), holdout: emptyAttachedTally() });\n332:   }\n333:   return byFamily.get(namespace);\n334: }\n335: \n336: function readHeadSha(workspaceRoot) {\n337:   const spawned = spawnSync('git', ['rev-parse', '--short', 'HEAD'], { cwd: workspaceRoot, encoding: 'utf8' });\n338:   return spawned.status === 0 ? spawned.stdout.trim() : 'unknown';\n339: }\n340: \n341: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n342: // 6. CORE LOGIC\n343: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n344: \n345: export async function scoreCommandCorpus(options = {}) {\n346:   const realEmbedding = options.realEmbedding === true;\n347:   const RealDate = Date;\n348:   // The projection needs an existing, empty cache directory, and that directory\n349:   // is scratch space this run owns. It is created before the `try` so a failure\n350:   // to create it has nothing left to remove, and removed in the `finally` so\n351:   // repeated captures do not leave one behind per run. A real capture reads the\n352:   // operator's own skill graph, so it owns no scratch directory and sweeps none.\n353:   const dbDir = realEmbedding ? null : mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'advisor-command-routing-'));\n354:   try {\n355:     // Environment projection. That empty directory makes the SQLite loader\n356:     // return null, so the projection is built from committed metadata alone,\n357:     // which is the only state a clean checkout can reproduce. A real capture reads\n358:     // the operator's own skill graph and embedder instead, so the three variables\n359:     // that select the projection are exactly the three it leaves unset.\n360:     if (!realEmbedding) {\n361:       process.env.SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DB_DIR = dbDir;\n362:       process.env.SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLE_BUILTIN_SEMANTIC = '1';\n363:       // Match the test-harness regime: the semantic-shadow lane substitutes\n364:       // deterministic fixture vectors under the harness flag (real embeddings are\n365:       // not reproducible in CI), so every scorer gate runs this way. The baseline\n366:       // must be captured under the same regime the ratchet re-scores it in.\n367:       process.env.VITEST = 'true';\n368:     }\n369:     process.env.SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_FORCE_LOCAL = '1';\n370:     process.env.PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE = '1';\n371:     delete process.env.SPECKIT_ADVISOR_LANE_WEIGHTS_JSON;\n372:     delete process.env.SPECKIT_ADVISOR_LANE_SHADOW_WEIGHTS_JSON;\n373:     delete process.env.SPECKIT_ADVISOR_BM25_LEXICAL_SHADOW;\n374:     // Private `local-*` skills linked in through this variable live outside the\n375:     // checkout, so a baseline measured on a clean checkout must not see them.\n376:     delete process.env.SPECKIT_PRIVATE_SKILL_ROOTS;\n377: \n378:     // Pin the scoring clock to midnight UTC on the inherited scoring date.\n379:     const capturedAt = readPinnedScoringDate();\n380:     const scoringInstant = Date.parse(`${capturedAt}T00:00:00Z`);\n381:     globalThis.Date = class extends RealDate {\n382:       constructor(...args) {\n383:         if (args.length === 0) super(scoringInstant);\n384:         else super(...args);\n385:       }\n386: \n387:       static now() {\n388:         return scoringInstant;\n389:       }\n390:     };\n391: \n392:     if (!existsSync(FUSION_MODULE)) {\n393:       throw new Error(`built scorer file missing: ${FUSION_MODULE}`);\n394:     }\n395:     for (const modulePath of [COMMAND_ATTACH_MODULE, COMPILED_ROUTING_FLAG_MODULE]) {\n396:       if (!existsSync(modulePath)) {\n397:         throw new Error(`built command-attach file missing: ${modulePath}`);\n398:       }\n399:     }\n400: \n401:     // Imported after the clock is pinned so the compiled scorer initializes\n402:     // against the pinned date, and only after it is known to exist so a missing\n403:     // build is reported as such rather than as a module-resolution failure.\n404:     const { scoreAdvisorPrompt } = await import(pathToFileURL(FUSION_MODULE).href);\n405:     const { findAdvisorWorkspaceRoot } = await import(pathToFileURL(WORKSPACE_ROOT_MODULE).href);\n406:     const { attachCommand } = await import(pathToFileURL(COMMAND_ATTACH_MODULE).href);\n407:     const { COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS } = await import(pathToFileURL(COMPILED_ROUTING_FLAG_MODULE).href);\n408:     const workspaceRoot = findAdvisorWorkspaceRoot(HERE, { maxDepth: 14, sentinel: SENTINEL });\n409: \n410:     // A missing front door would make every spawn fail and report the same zero\n411:     // as a hub with nothing to attach, so it fails the capture instead.\n412:     if (!existsSync(resolve(workspaceRoot, COMPILED_ROUTE_CLI))) {\n413:       throw new Error(`compiled route front door missing: ${resolve(workspaceRoot, COMPILED_ROUTE_CLI)}`);\n414:     }\n415: \n416:     // A build older than its source scores code that no longer exists, so its\n417:     // numbers would describe a scorer nobody can read. Refuse it, and likewise\n418:     // a build whose freshness cannot be assessed.\n419:     const { checkPackageFreshness } = createRequire(import.meta.url)(DIST_FRESHNESS_MODULE);\n420:     const freshness = checkPackageFreshness('system-skill-advisor/runtime', { workspaceRoot });\n421:     if (freshness.status !== 'fresh') {\n422:       throw new Error(freshness.message);\n423:     }\n424: \n425:     // Real scoring is the only mode that reads this machine, so it is the only one\n426:     // that has to prove the machine can answer before walking the corpus. Without\n427:     // the flag nothing here runs and the prompt embedding is the direct call below.\n428:     const embedPrompt = realEmbedding ? await prepareRealEmbeddingScoring() : null;\n429: \n430:     const commandNames = readCommandFileNames(workspaceRoot);\n431:     const commandSet = new Set(commandNames);\n432:     const { bridgeCounts, idToCommand } = readBridgeMap(BRIDGES_JSON);\n433:     const corpus = readCorpus(CORPUS_JSONL);\n434: \n435:     // A label naming a command that does not exist would silently score as a\n436:     // permanent miss, so it fails the capture instead of deflating a metric.\n437:     for (const row of corpus) {\n438:       if (row.expected_command === null || row.expected_command === undefined) continue;\n439:       if (!commandSet.has(row.expected_command)) {\n440:         throw new Error(`corpus row ${row.id} expects ${row.expected_command}, which is not a command file under ${resolve(workspaceRoot, COMMANDS_DIR)}`);\n441:       }\n442:     }\n443: \n444:     const perCommand = new Map();\n445:     for (const command of commandNames) {\n446:       perCommand.set(command, { typed: emptyTally(), plain: { tune: emptyTally(), holdout: emptyTally() } });\n447:     }\n448: \n449:     const typed = emptyTally();\n450:     const plain = { tune: emptyTally(), holdout: emptyTally() };\n451:     let falseOfferCount = 0;\n452:     let falseOfferTotal = 0;\n453:     let slashReachedTop3 = 0;\n454:     let slashNamedTotal = 0;\n455:     let slashOfferedOnPartial = 0;\n456:     let slashPartialTotal = 0;\n457:     let agreementCorrect = 0;\n458:     let agreementTotal = 0;\n459:     // Per-row results sit beside the totals because a total can hold still\n460:     // while one row is gained and another lost, and a changed label has to\n461:     // surface under the id of the row it belongs to.\n462:     const rows = {};\n463:     // Attached reach: what a hub's own route adds on the same rows, split the\n464:     // same way, plus the rows that had to stay silent and the ties a bridge\n465:     // leaves with its own owner.\n466:     const attachedRows = {};\n467:     const plainAttached = { tune: emptyAttachedTally(), holdout: emptyAttachedTally() };\n468:     const plainAttachedByFamily = new Map();\n469:     const sameOwnerAmbiguous = [];\n470:     let falseOfferAttached = 0;\n471: \n472:     for (const row of corpus) {\n473:       // The recommend path supplies the semantic lane's prompt vector through this\n474:       // wrapper, so a real capture threads the same path rather than scoring with\n475:       // the lane blind. Under the fixture regime the lane derives its own\n476:       // deterministic vector and the direct call is the whole behavior.\n477:       const score = () => scoreAdvisorPrompt(row.prompt, { workspaceRoot });\n478:       const result = embedPrompt === null ? score() : await embedPrompt(row.prompt, score);\n479:       const ids = recommendationIds(result);\n480:       const landed = ids.map((id) => commandOf(idToCommand, id));\n481:       const expected = row.expected_command ?? null;\n482:       const top1Hit = expected !== null && landed[0] === expected;\n483:       const top3Hit = expected !== null && landed.includes(expected);\n484:       const offered = landed.some((command) => command !== null);\n485:       const isPlainSplit = row.row_type === 'plain' && (row.split === 'tune' || row.split === 'holdout');\n486: \n487:       // A hub can name the slash command its route would take, which is reach a\n488:       // reader otherwise gets only by translating the workflow mode by hand.\n489:       const topId = ids[0];\n490:       const attached = topId !== undefined && COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS.has(topId)\n491:         ? attachCommand(topId, readCompiledRoute(workspaceRoot, topId, row.prompt), row.prompt)\n492:         : undefined;\n493:       const attachedHit = attached !== undefined && attached === expected && !top1Hit;\n494: \n495:       if (row.row_type === 'typed') addRow(typed, top1Hit, top3Hit);\n496:       if (isPlainSplit) {\n497:         addRow(plain[row.split], top1Hit, top3Hit);\n498:         addAttachedRow(plainAttached[row.split], top1Hit, attachedHit);\n499:         const family = attachedFamilyTally(plainAttachedByFamily, expected);\n500:         if (family) addAttachedRow(family[row.split], top1Hit, attachedHit);\n501:       }\n502:       if (attached !== undefined) attachedRows[row.id] = attached;\n503:       if (row.row_type === 'typed' && result?.ambiguous === true && isBridgeOwnerPair(idToCommand, ids, expected)) {\n504:         sameOwnerAmbiguous.push(row.id);\n505:       }\n506: \n507:       const commandTally = expected === null ? undefined : perCommand.get(expected);\n508:       if (commandTally) {\n509:         if (row.row_type === 'typed') addRow(commandTally.typed, top1Hit, top3Hit);\n510:         else if (isPlainSplit) addRow(commandTally.plain[row.split], top1Hit, top3Hit);\n511:       }\n512: \n513:       rows[row.id] = { expected_command: expected, top: ids[0] ?? null, top1: top1Hit, top3: top3Hit, offered };\n514: \n515:       const isSlashVariant = row.row_type === 'slash-variant';\n516:       // Silence is the requirement for a near miss, a prompt that must not\n517:       // trigger a command, and a partial slash mention that names no command.\n518:       const wantsSilence = row.row_type === 'must-not-start'\n519:         || row.row_type === 'near-miss'\n520:         || (isSlashVariant && expected === null);\n521:       if (wantsSilence) {\n522:         falseOfferTotal += 1;\n523:         if (offered) falseOfferCount += 1;\n524:         if (attached !== undefined) falseOfferAttached += 1;\n525:       }\n526: \n527:       if (isSlashVariant) {\n528:         if (expected === null) {\n529:           slashPartialTotal += 1;\n530:           if (offered) slashOfferedOnPartial += 1;\n531:         } else {\n532:           slashNamedTotal += 1;\n533:           if (top3Hit) slashReachedTop3 += 1;\n534:         }\n535:       }\n536: \n537:       // A bridge offered below a skill is correct when the skill that outranked\n538:       // it is the bridge's own owner: the command stays reachable without the\n539:       // bridge displacing the skill the prompt actually asked for.\n540:       if (!idToCommand.has(ids[0])) {\n541:         const laterBridgeId = ids.slice(1).find((id) => idToCommand.has(id));\n542:         if (laterBridgeId !== undefined) {\n543:           agreementTotal += 1;\n544:           if (idToCommand.get(laterBridgeId).skillId === ids[0]) agreementCorrect += 1;\n545:         }\n546:       }\n547:     }\n548: \n549:     const commands = {};\n550:     for (const command of [...perCommand.keys()].sort()) {\n551:       commands[command] = perCommand.get(command);\n552:     }\n553: \n554:     const reachableCommands = new Set();\n555:     for (const value of idToCommand.values()) reachableCommands.add(value.command);\n556:     const unreachable = commandNames.filter((command) => !reachableCommands.has(command)).sort();\n557: \n558:     const plainByFamily = {};\n559:     for (const namespace of [...plainAttachedByFamily.keys()].sort()) {\n560:       const family = plainAttachedByFamily.get(namespace);\n561:       plainByFamily[namespace] = {\n562:         tune: attachedTallyJson(family.tune),\n563:         holdout: attachedTallyJson(family.holdout),\n564:       };\n565:     }\n566: \n567:     return {\n568:       schemaVersion: 1,\n569:       capturedAt,\n570:       capturedAtSha: readHeadSha(workspaceRoot),\n571:       corpusSha256: sha256File(CORPUS_JSONL),\n572:       bridgeCounts,\n573:       metrics: {\n574:         typed_recall_at_1: { correct: typed.top1, total: typed.rows },\n575:         typed_reach_top3: { correct: typed.top3, total: typed.rows },\n576:         plain_recall_at_1: {\n577:           tune: { correct: plain.tune.top1, total: plain.tune.rows },\n578:           holdout: { correct: plain.holdout.top1, total: plain.holdout.rows },\n579:         },\n580:         plain_reach_top3: {\n581:           tune: { correct: plain.tune.top3, total: plain.tune.rows },\n582:           holdout: { correct: plain.holdout.top3, total: plain.holdout.rows },\n583:         },\n584:         false_offer: { count: falseOfferCount, total: falseOfferTotal },\n585:         slash_variant: {\n586:           reached_top3: slashReachedTop3,\n587:           named_total: slashNamedTotal,\n588:           offered_on_partial: slashOfferedOnPartial,\n589:           partial_total: slashPartialTotal,\n590:         },\n591:         agreement: { correct: agreementCorrect, total: agreementTotal },\n592:       },\n593:       unreachable,\n594:       commands,\n595:       rows,\n596:       attachedMetrics: {\n597:         plain: {\n598:           tune: attachedTallyJson(plainAttached.tune),\n599:           holdout: attachedTallyJson(plainAttached.holdout),\n600:         },\n601:         plain_by_family: plainByFamily,\n602:         false_offer_attached: { count: falseOfferAttached, total: falseOfferTotal },\n603:         typed_same_owner_ambiguous: sameOwnerAmbiguous.sort(),\n604:         attachedRows,\n605:       },\n606:     };\n607:   } finally {\n608:     globalThis.Date = RealDate;\n609:     // Removal comes last, after every read that needs the directory has\n610:     // finished; `force` keeps an already-swept directory from turning a\n611:     // completed capture into a failure.\n612:     if (dbDir !== null) rmSync(dbDir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n613:   }\n614: }\n615: \n616: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n617: // 7. CLI ENTRY\n618: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n619: \n620: if (process.argv[1] && import.meta.url === pathToFileURL(resolve(process.argv[1])).href) {\n621:   rejectUnknownOptions(import.meta.url, ['--write', '--real-embedding']);\n622: \n623:   const realEmbedding = process.argv.includes('--real-embedding');\n624:   // The committed baseline has to be reproducible on a clean checkout, so it may\n625:   // only come from the fixture regime. A real-embedding run measures this machine's\n626:   // embedder and graph, which is a live probe rather than a ratchet.\n627:   if (realEmbedding && process.argv.includes('--write')) {\n628:     process.stderr.write('capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs: --write cannot be combined with --real-embedding; the committed baseline is captured under the fixture regime only\\n');\n629:     process.exit(2);\n630:   }\n631: \n632:   try {\n633:     const baseline = await scoreCommandCorpus({ realEmbedding });\n634:     const serialized = `${JSON.stringify(baseline, null, 2)}\\n`;\n635:     if (process.argv.includes('--write')) {\n636:       writeFileSync(OUTPUT_JSON, serialized, 'utf8');\n637:       console.log(`wrote baseline -> ${OUTPUT_JSON}`);\n638:     }\n639:     console.log(serialized);\n640:   } catch (error) {\n641:     process.stderr.write(`capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs: ${error.message}\\n`);\n642:     process.exit(2);\n643:   }\n644: }","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs, lines 1-644\n1: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n2: // MODULE: COMMAND ROUTING BASELINE CAPTURE\n3: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n4: //\n5: // Captures how the built scorer routes the command-prompt corpus under the\n6: // reproducible filesystem projection (empty SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DB_DIR →\n7: // SQLite loader returns null → projection built purely from committed graph\n8: // metadata). It answers the questions command surfacing has to hold to: a\n9: // prompt that names a slash command reaches that command's bridge, a prompt\n10: // that must stay quiet offers no command at all, a partial slash mention is\n11: // not routed as if it were complete, and a bridge ranked below the top skill\n12: // is owned by that skill.\n13: //\n14: // It also measures what the compiled route adds on top of that: a hub can name\n15: // the slash command its own routing would take, which reaches a reader who never\n16: // types a slash command. That reach is reported under its own key so every\n17: // existing number keeps the value and the comparability it had before.\n18: //\n19: // The result is the ratchet baseline: the gate re-scores live under the same\n20: // environment and holds every count to it, so any scoring or bridge-metadata\n21: // change that moves a number forces a conscious re-capture rather than\n22: // drifting silently. `corpusSha256` pins the baseline to the exact corpus it\n23: // was captured against, so a corpus edit changes the hash and forces\n24: // re-baselining.\n25: //\n26: // The same corpus can also be scored against the operator's own skill graph and\n27: // embedder under `--real-embedding`, which is a live probe of this machine rather\n28: // than a reproducible projection. Its numbers describe an environment, so it never\n29: // writes the baseline and never runs under the fixture regime the ratchet replays.\n30: //\n31: // Setup runs on call, never on import: the environment projection, the clock\n32: // pin and the scorer import all live inside scoreCommandCorpus(), so a harness\n33: // may import this module and score in-process without inheriting either.\n34: //\n35: // Usage:\n36: //   node capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs            # print JSON to stdout\n37: //   node capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs --write    # also write the baseline\n38: //   node capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs --real-embedding   # score live vectors\n39: \n40: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n41: // 1. IMPORTS\n42: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n43: \n44: import { execFileSync, spawnSync } from 'node:child_process';\n45: import { createHash } from 'node:crypto';\n46: import { existsSync, mkdtempSync, readdirSync, readFileSync, rmSync, writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';\n47: import { createRequire } from 'node:module';\n48: import { tmpdir } from 'node:os';\n49: import { dirname, join, resolve } from 'node:path';\n50: import { fileURLToPath, pathToFileURL } from 'node:url';\n51: import { rejectUnknownOptions } from './reject-unknown-options.mjs';\n52: \n53: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n54: // 2. CONSTANTS\n55: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n56: \n57: const HERE = dirname(fileURLToPath(import.meta.url));\n58: const CORPUS_JSONL = resolve(HERE, 'command-prompts.jsonl');\n59: const BRIDGES_JSON = resolve(HERE, '../command-bridges/command-bridges.generated.json');\n60: const SCORER_EVAL_BASELINE_JSON = resolve(HERE, 'scorer-eval-baseline.json');\n61: const OUTPUT_JSON = resolve(HERE, 'command-routing-baseline.json');\n62: const DIST = resolve(HERE, '../../dist/runtime');\n63: const FUSION_MODULE = resolve(DIST, 'lib/scorer/fusion.js');\n64: const WORKSPACE_ROOT_MODULE = resolve(DIST, 'lib/utils/workspace-root.js');\n65: const COMMAND_ATTACH_MODULE = resolve(DIST, 'lib/command-attach.js');\n66: const COMPILED_ROUTING_FLAG_MODULE = resolve(DIST, 'lib/compiled-routing-flag.js');\n67: const SEMANTIC_SHADOW_MODULE = resolve(DIST, 'lib/scorer/lanes/semantic-shadow.js');\n68: const SKILL_GRAPH_DB_MODULE = resolve(DIST, 'lib/skill-graph/skill-graph-db.js');\n69: const DIST_FRESHNESS_MODULE = resolve(HERE, '../../../../system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/lib/dist-freshness.cjs');\n70: \n71: const SENTINEL = '.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/SKILL.md';\n72: const COMMANDS_DIR = '.skilled/commands';\n73: // Only the folder-level README is a document; a lowercase `readme.md` shipped\n74: // beside the commands is itself a command, so the skip is case-sensitive.\n75: const COMMAND_DOC_SKIP = 'README.md';\n76: const COMMAND_DOC_SUFFIX = '.md';\n77: // The compiled route is asked for through the public front door a hub's SKILL.md\n78: // calls, so this measures the decision the runtime actually serves rather than a\n79: // second derivation of it that could disagree with the first.\n80: const COMPILED_ROUTE_CLI = '.skilled/bin/compiled-route.cjs';\n81: const COMPILED_ROUTE_TIMEOUT_MS = 5000;\n82: \n83: // A real capture talks to the same embedder the recommend path does, so the probe\n84: // asks that daemon directly and names the model family the routing vectors come\n85: // from. The command vectors sit beside the skill vectors in the skill graph, in a\n86: // table whose name carries the embedder dimension.\n87: const EMBEDDER_TAGS_URL = 'http://localhost:11434/api/tags';\n88: const EMBEDDER_MODEL_PREFIX = 'nomic-embed-text';\n89: const EMBEDDER_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS = 5000;\n90: const COMMAND_VECTOR_TABLE_PREFIX = 'vec_cmd_';\n91: \n92: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n93: // 3. RUNTIME SETUP\n94: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n95: //\n96: // Only the value lookups live here; the mutations themselves are deferred into\n97: // scoreCommandCorpus() so importing this module has no side effects.\n98: \n99: function isCalendarDate(value) {\n100:   if (!/^\\d{4}-\\d{2}-\\d{2}$/.test(value)) return false;\n101:   const parsed = new Date(`${value}T00:00:00Z`);\n102:   return !Number.isNaN(parsed.getTime()) && parsed.toISOString().slice(0, 10) === value;\n103: }\n104: \n105: // The derived lane halves each skill's score every 90 days of age, measured\n106: // from its derived.generated_at to the scoring clock (floored at 0.1), so\n107: // scores move with the calendar. The scoring date is therefore inherited from\n108: // the scorer-eval baseline and pinned, never read from the wall clock: a\n109: // recapture on any day replays the recorded date and reproduces the same\n110: // numbers, which keeps the two baselines directly comparable.\n111: function readPinnedScoringDate() {\n112:   let recorded;\n113:   try {\n114:     recorded = JSON.parse(readFileSync(SCORER_EVAL_BASELINE_JSON, 'utf8')).capturedAt;\n115:   } catch {\n116:     recorded = undefined;\n117:   }\n118:   if (typeof recorded !== 'string' || !isCalendarDate(recorded)) {\n119:     throw new Error(`scorer-eval baseline has no pinned scoring date (capturedAt): ${SCORER_EVAL_BASELINE_JSON}`);\n120:   }\n121:   return recorded;\n122: }\n123: \n124: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n125: // 4. REAL EMBEDDING PREFLIGHT\n126: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n127: //\n128: // A real capture scores whatever this machine's cosine lane can see, so both\n129: // halves of that lane are proved before a single row is scored: an embedder that\n130: // answers and a populated command vector table. Each half is named as itself when\n131: // it is missing, because a silent cosine lane reads exactly like a scoring change.\n132: \n133: async function assertEmbedderAnswers() {\n134:   const listed = [];\n135:   try {\n136:     const response = await fetch(EMBEDDER_TAGS_URL, { signal: AbortSignal.timeout(EMBEDDER_PROBE_TIMEOUT_MS) });\n137:     if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);\n138:     const payload = await response.json();\n139:     for (const model of Array.isArray(payload?.models) ? payload.models : []) {\n140:       if (typeof model?.name === 'string') listed.push(model.name);\n141:     }\n142:   } catch (error) {\n143:     const message = error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error);\n144:     throw new Error(`real embedding needs a live embedder: GET ${EMBEDDER_TAGS_URL} failed (${message})`);\n145:   }\n146:   // The stored vectors were built with this model family, so a daemon serving some\n147:   // other model cannot answer them no matter how well the dimensions line up.\n148:   if (!listed.some((name) => name.startsWith(EMBEDDER_MODEL_PREFIX))) {\n149:     throw new Error(`real embedding needs an embedder serving ${EMBEDDER_MODEL_PREFIX}*: GET ${EMBEDDER_TAGS_URL} lists ${listed.length > 0 ? listed.join(', ') : 'no models'}`);\n150:   }\n151: }\n152: \n153: function assertCommandVectorsPresent(database, databasePath) {\n154:   if (!database) {\n155:     throw new Error(`real embedding needs command vectors: the skill graph database is absent (${databasePath})`);\n156:   }\n157:   // GLOB keeps the trailing underscore of the prefix literal, so the match is the\n158:   // `vec_cmd_<dim>` family alone rather than any name shaped like it.\n159:   const tables = database\n160:     .prepare(\"SELECT name FROM sqlite_master WHERE type = 'table' AND name GLOB ?\")\n161:     .all(`${COMMAND_VECTOR_TABLE_PREFIX}*`)\n162:     .map((table) => table.name);\n163:   if (tables.length === 0) {\n164:     throw new Error(`real embedding needs command vectors: no ${COMMAND_VECTOR_TABLE_PREFIX}* table in ${databasePath}`);\n165:   }\n166:   const populated = tables.filter((name) => database.prepare(`SELECT COUNT(*) AS rowCount FROM \"${name}\"`).get().rowCount > 0);\n167:   if (populated.length === 0) {\n168:     throw new Error(`real embedding needs command vectors: ${tables.join(', ')} has no rows in ${databasePath}`);\n169:   }\n170: }\n171: \n172: // The wrapper, the embedder and the command vectors are resolved together because\n173: // a real capture needs all three or it measures nothing worth reporting.\n174: async function prepareRealEmbeddingScoring() {\n175:   if (!existsSync(SEMANTIC_SHADOW_MODULE)) {\n176:     throw new Error(`built semantic-shadow file missing: ${SEMANTIC_SHADOW_MODULE}`);\n177:   }\n178:   const { withSemanticShadowPromptEmbedding } = await import(pathToFileURL(SEMANTIC_SHADOW_MODULE).href);\n179:   const { getDbReadOnly, getSkillGraphDbPath } = await import(pathToFileURL(SKILL_GRAPH_DB_MODULE).href);\n180:   await assertEmbedderAnswers();\n181:   assertCommandVectorsPresent(getDbReadOnly(), getSkillGraphDbPath());\n182:   return withSemanticShadowPromptEmbedding;\n183: }\n184: \n185: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n186: // 5. HELPERS\n187: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n188: \n189: function sha256File(path) {\n190:   return `sha256:${createHash('sha256').update(readFileSync(path)).digest('hex')}`;\n191: }\n192: \n193: // Line numbers are reported as the physical line a bad row sits on, so a\n194: // parse failure points at the line to repair rather than at a row ordinal.\n195: function readCorpus(path) {\n196:   if (!existsSync(path)) throw new Error(`corpus file missing: ${path}`);\n197:   const lines = readFileSync(path, 'utf8').split('\\n');\n198:   const rows = [];\n199:   for (let index = 0; index < lines.length; index += 1) {\n200:     const line = lines[index].trim();\n201:     if (line === '') continue;\n202:     try {\n203:       rows.push(JSON.parse(line));\n204:     } catch (error) {\n205:       throw new Error(`invalid JSON on corpus line ${index + 1} of ${path}: ${error.message}`);\n206:     }\n207:   }\n208:   if (rows.length === 0) throw new Error(`corpus file empty: ${path}`);\n209:   return rows;\n210: }\n211: \n212: // Commands live exactly two levels deep, `<namespace>/<name>.md`, and become\n213: // `/<namespace>:<name>`. Anything directly in the commands root is a document\n214: // rather than a command, and deeper nesting is a command's own assets.\n215: function readCommandFileNames(workspaceRoot) {\n216:   const commandsRoot = resolve(workspaceRoot, COMMANDS_DIR);\n217:   const commands = [];\n218:   for (const namespace of readdirSync(commandsRoot, { withFileTypes: true })) {\n219:     if (!namespace.isDirectory()) continue;\n220:     for (const file of readdirSync(join(commandsRoot, namespace.name), { withFileTypes: true })) {\n221:       if (!file.isFile()) continue;\n222:       if (!file.name.endsWith(COMMAND_DOC_SUFFIX) || file.name === COMMAND_DOC_SKIP) continue;\n223:       commands.push(`/${namespace.name}:${file.name.slice(0, -COMMAND_DOC_SUFFIX.length)}`);\n224:     }\n225:   }\n226:   return commands.sort();\n227: }\n228: \n229: // The TypeScript inventory id is what the scorer actually emits, and it differs\n230: // from the human command name, so the bridge id is the map key. A command with\n231: // only the Python runtime enabled has no TypeScript id to emit and is reported\n232: // as unreachable instead.\n233: function readBridgeMap(path) {\n234:   const bridges = JSON.parse(readFileSync(path, 'utf8'));\n235:   const idToCommand = new Map();\n236:   for (const entry of bridges.entries ?? []) {\n237:     const typescript = entry.runtime?.typescript;\n238:     if (typescript?.enabled !== true || typeof typescript.id !== 'string') continue;\n239:     idToCommand.set(typescript.id, { command: entry.command, skillId: entry.skillId });\n240:   }\n241:   return { bridgeCounts: bridges.counts ?? {}, idToCommand };\n242: }\n243: \n244: function commandOf(idToCommand, id) {\n245:   return idToCommand.get(id)?.command ?? null;\n246: }\n247: \n248: // The route arrives from a separate process, so a spawn that fails, times out or\n249: // prints anything unparsable yields no route: a broken read must degrade to \"no\n250: // command offered\" rather than to an invented one.\n251: function readCompiledRoute(workspaceRoot, hubId, prompt) {\n252:   try {\n253:     const stdout = execFileSync('node', [join(workspaceRoot, COMPILED_ROUTE_CLI), '--hub', hubId, '--prompt-stdin'], {\n254:       cwd: workspaceRoot,\n255:       input: prompt,\n256:       encoding: 'utf8',\n257:       timeout: COMPILED_ROUTE_TIMEOUT_MS,\n258:       stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'ignore'],\n259:     });\n260:     const route = JSON.parse(stdout);\n261:     return typeof route === 'object' && route !== null ? route : undefined;\n262:   } catch {\n263:     return undefined;\n264:   }\n265: }\n266: \n267: // A bridge that outranks nothing still stays reachable: when the two leaders are\n268: // a bridge and the skill that owns it, the reader gets the command without the\n269: // bridge displacing the skill the prompt actually asked for. The order is\n270: // irrelevant because the scorer's tie-break says nothing about ownership.\n271: function isBridgeOwnerPair(idToCommand, ids, expected) {\n272:   const [first, second] = ids;\n273:   if (first === undefined || second === undefined) return false;\n274:   const leading = idToCommand.get(first);\n275:   if (leading && leading.command === expected && leading.skillId === second) return true;\n276:   const trailing = idToCommand.get(second);\n277:   return Boolean(trailing && trailing.command === expected && trailing.skillId === first);\n278: }\n279: \n280: // The scorer reports ranked recommendations when the projection produced a\n281: // list, and only a top pick otherwise; both shapes have to be scorable.\n282: function recommendationIds(result) {\n283:   if (Array.isArray(result?.recommendations)) {\n284:     return result.recommendations.slice(0, 3).map((recommendation) => recommendation.skill);\n285:   }\n286:   return result?.topSkill === null || result?.topSkill === undefined ? [] : [result.topSkill];\n287: }\n288: \n289: function emptyTally() {\n290:   return { rows: 0, top1: 0, top3: 0 };\n291: }\n292: \n293: function addRow(tally, top1Hit, top3Hit) {\n294:   tally.rows += 1;\n295:   if (top1Hit) tally.top1 += 1;\n296:   if (top3Hit) tally.top3 += 1;\n297: }\n298: \n299: // Attached reach is kept apart from bridge reach and only ever counts a row the\n300: // ranked bridge did not already land, so `own` and `attached` partition the rows\n301: // and their sum is real reach rather than one row claimed twice.\n302: function emptyAttachedTally() {\n303:   return { own: 0, attached: 0, total: 0 };\n304: }\n305: \n306: function addAttachedRow(tally, ownHit, attachedHit) {\n307:   tally.total += 1;\n308:   if (ownHit) tally.own += 1;\n309:   if (attachedHit) tally.attached += 1;\n310: }\n311: \n312: function attachedTallyJson(tally) {\n313:   return { own: tally.own, attached: tally.attached, combined: tally.own + tally.attached, total: tally.total };\n314: }\n315: \n316: // Commands are named `<namespace>:<name>`, so the namespace is the workflow\n317: // family a reach number belongs to.\n318: function commandNamespace(command) {\n319:   if (typeof command !== 'string' || !command.startsWith('/')) return null;\n320:   const colon = command.indexOf(':');\n321:   return colon > 1 ? command.slice(1, colon) : null;\n322: }\n323: \n324: // Individual rows roll up to a family only when their command names one, so a\n325: // command outside the `<namespace>:<name>` grammar still counts in the split\n326: // total without inventing a family for itself.\n327: function attachedFamilyTally(byFamily, command) {\n328:   const namespace = commandNamespace(command);\n329:   if (namespace === null) return undefined;\n330:   if (!byFamily.has(namespace)) {\n331:     byFamily.set(namespace, { tune: emptyAttachedTally(), holdout: emptyAttachedTally() });\n332:   }\n333:   return byFamily.get(namespace);\n334: }\n335: \n336: function readHeadSha(workspaceRoot) {\n337:   const spawned = spawnSync('git', ['rev-parse', '--short', 'HEAD'], { cwd: workspaceRoot, encoding: 'utf8' });\n338:   return spawned.status === 0 ? spawned.stdout.trim() : 'unknown';\n339: }\n340: \n341: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n342: // 6. CORE LOGIC\n343: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n344: \n345: export async function scoreCommandCorpus(options = {}) {\n346:   const realEmbedding = options.realEmbedding === true;\n347:   const RealDate = Date;\n348:   // The projection needs an existing, empty cache directory, and that directory\n349:   // is scratch space this run owns. It is created before the `try` so a failure\n350:   // to create it has nothing left to remove, and removed in the `finally` so\n351:   // repeated captures do not leave one behind per run. A real capture reads the\n352:   // operator's own skill graph, so it owns no scratch directory and sweeps none.\n353:   const dbDir = realEmbedding ? null : mkdtempSync(join(tmpdir(), 'advisor-command-routing-'));\n354:   try {\n355:     // Environment projection. That empty directory makes the SQLite loader\n356:     // return null, so the projection is built from committed metadata alone,\n357:     // which is the only state a clean checkout can reproduce. A real capture reads\n358:     // the operator's own skill graph and embedder instead, so the three variables\n359:     // that select the projection are exactly the three it leaves unset.\n360:     if (!realEmbedding) {\n361:       process.env.SYSTEM_SKILL_ADVISOR_DB_DIR = dbDir;\n362:       process.env.SKILL_ADVISOR_DISABLE_BUILTIN_SEMANTIC = '1';\n363:       // Match the test-harness regime: the semantic-shadow lane substitutes\n364:       // deterministic fixture vectors under the harness flag (real embeddings are\n365:       // not reproducible in CI), so every scorer gate runs this way. The baseline\n366:       // must be captured under the same regime the ratchet re-scores it in.\n367:       process.env.VITEST = 'true';\n368:     }\n369:     process.env.SPECKIT_SKILL_ADVISOR_FORCE_LOCAL = '1';\n370:     process.env.PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE = '1';\n371:     delete process.env.SPECKIT_ADVISOR_LANE_WEIGHTS_JSON;\n372:     delete process.env.SPECKIT_ADVISOR_LANE_SHADOW_WEIGHTS_JSON;\n373:     delete process.env.SPECKIT_ADVISOR_BM25_LEXICAL_SHADOW;\n374:     // Private `local-*` skills linked in through this variable live outside the\n375:     // checkout, so a baseline measured on a clean checkout must not see them.\n376:     delete process.env.SPECKIT_PRIVATE_SKILL_ROOTS;\n377: \n378:     // Pin the scoring clock to midnight UTC on the inherited scoring date.\n379:     const capturedAt = readPinnedScoringDate();\n380:     const scoringInstant = Date.parse(`${capturedAt}T00:00:00Z`);\n381:     globalThis.Date = class extends RealDate {\n382:       constructor(...args) {\n383:         if (args.length === 0) super(scoringInstant);\n384:         else super(...args);\n385:       }\n386: \n387:       static now() {\n388:         return scoringInstant;\n389:       }\n390:     };\n391: \n392:     if (!existsSync(FUSION_MODULE)) {\n393:       throw new Error(`built scorer file missing: ${FUSION_MODULE}`);\n394:     }\n395:     for (const modulePath of [COMMAND_ATTACH_MODULE, COMPILED_ROUTING_FLAG_MODULE]) {\n396:       if (!existsSync(modulePath)) {\n397:         throw new Error(`built command-attach file missing: ${modulePath}`);\n398:       }\n399:     }\n400: \n401:     // Imported after the clock is pinned so the compiled scorer initializes\n402:     // against the pinned date, and only after it is known to exist so a missing\n403:     // build is reported as such rather than as a module-resolution failure.\n404:     const { scoreAdvisorPrompt } = await import(pathToFileURL(FUSION_MODULE).href);\n405:     const { findAdvisorWorkspaceRoot } = await import(pathToFileURL(WORKSPACE_ROOT_MODULE).href);\n406:     const { attachCommand } = await import(pathToFileURL(COMMAND_ATTACH_MODULE).href);\n407:     const { COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS } = await import(pathToFileURL(COMPILED_ROUTING_FLAG_MODULE).href);\n408:     const workspaceRoot = findAdvisorWorkspaceRoot(HERE, { maxDepth: 14, sentinel: SENTINEL });\n409: \n410:     // A missing front door would make every spawn fail and report the same zero\n411:     // as a hub with nothing to attach, so it fails the capture instead.\n412:     if (!existsSync(resolve(workspaceRoot, COMPILED_ROUTE_CLI))) {\n413:       throw new Error(`compiled route front door missing: ${resolve(workspaceRoot, COMPILED_ROUTE_CLI)}`);\n414:     }\n415: \n416:     // A build older than its source scores code that no longer exists, so its\n417:     // numbers would describe a scorer nobody can read. Refuse it, and likewise\n418:     // a build whose freshness cannot be assessed.\n419:     const { checkPackageFreshness } = createRequire(import.meta.url)(DIST_FRESHNESS_MODULE);\n420:     const freshness = checkPackageFreshness('system-skill-advisor/runtime', { workspaceRoot });\n421:     if (freshness.status !== 'fresh') {\n422:       throw new Error(freshness.message);\n423:     }\n424: \n425:     // Real scoring is the only mode that reads this machine, so it is the only one\n426:     // that has to prove the machine can answer before walking the corpus. Without\n427:     // the flag nothing here runs and the prompt embedding is the direct call below.\n428:     const embedPrompt = realEmbedding ? await prepareRealEmbeddingScoring() : null;\n429: \n430:     const commandNames = readCommandFileNames(workspaceRoot);\n431:     const commandSet = new Set(commandNames);\n432:     const { bridgeCounts, idToCommand } = readBridgeMap(BRIDGES_JSON);\n433:     const corpus = readCorpus(CORPUS_JSONL);\n434: \n435:     // A label naming a command that does not exist would silently score as a\n436:     // permanent miss, so it fails the capture instead of deflating a metric.\n437:     for (const row of corpus) {\n438:       if (row.expected_command === null || row.expected_command === undefined) continue;\n439:       if (!commandSet.has(row.expected_command)) {\n440:         throw new Error(`corpus row ${row.id} expects ${row.expected_command}, which is not a command file under ${resolve(workspaceRoot, COMMANDS_DIR)}`);\n441:       }\n442:     }\n443: \n444:     const perCommand = new Map();\n445:     for (const command of commandNames) {\n446:       perCommand.set(command, { typed: emptyTally(), plain: { tune: emptyTally(), holdout: emptyTally() } });\n447:     }\n448: \n449:     const typed = emptyTally();\n450:     const plain = { tune: emptyTally(), holdout: emptyTally() };\n451:     let falseOfferCount = 0;\n452:     let falseOfferTotal = 0;\n453:     let slashReachedTop3 = 0;\n454:     let slashNamedTotal = 0;\n455:     let slashOfferedOnPartial = 0;\n456:     let slashPartialTotal = 0;\n457:     let agreementCorrect = 0;\n458:     let agreementTotal = 0;\n459:     // Per-row results sit beside the totals because a total can hold still\n460:     // while one row is gained and another lost, and a changed label has to\n461:     // surface under the id of the row it belongs to.\n462:     const rows = {};\n463:     // Attached reach: what a hub's own route adds on the same rows, split the\n464:     // same way, plus the rows that had to stay silent and the ties a bridge\n465:     // leaves with its own owner.\n466:     const attachedRows = {};\n467:     const plainAttached = { tune: emptyAttachedTally(), holdout: emptyAttachedTally() };\n468:     const plainAttachedByFamily = new Map();\n469:     const sameOwnerAmbiguous = [];\n470:     let falseOfferAttached = 0;\n471: \n472:     for (const row of corpus) {\n473:       // The recommend path supplies the semantic lane's prompt vector through this\n474:       // wrapper, so a real capture threads the same path rather than scoring with\n475:       // the lane blind. Under the fixture regime the lane derives its own\n476:       // deterministic vector and the direct call is the whole behavior.\n477:       const score = () => scoreAdvisorPrompt(row.prompt, { workspaceRoot });\n478:       const result = embedPrompt === null ? score() : await embedPrompt(row.prompt, score);\n479:       const ids = recommendationIds(result);\n480:       const landed = ids.map((id) => commandOf(idToCommand, id));\n481:       const expected = row.expected_command ?? null;\n482:       const top1Hit = expected !== null && landed[0] === expected;\n483:       const top3Hit = expected !== null && landed.includes(expected);\n484:       const offered = landed.some((command) => command !== null);\n485:       const isPlainSplit = row.row_type === 'plain' && (row.split === 'tune' || row.split === 'holdout');\n486: \n487:       // A hub can name the slash command its route would take, which is reach a\n488:       // reader otherwise gets only by translating the workflow mode by hand.\n489:       const topId = ids[0];\n490:       const attached = topId !== undefined && COMPILED_ROUTING_HUBS.has(topId)\n491:         ? attachCommand(topId, readCompiledRoute(workspaceRoot, topId, row.prompt), row.prompt)\n492:         : undefined;\n493:       const attachedHit = attached !== undefined && attached === expected && !top1Hit;\n494: \n495:       if (row.row_type === 'typed') addRow(typed, top1Hit, top3Hit);\n496:       if (isPlainSplit) {\n497:         addRow(plain[row.split], top1Hit, top3Hit);\n498:         addAttachedRow(plainAttached[row.split], top1Hit, attachedHit);\n499:         const family = attachedFamilyTally(plainAttachedByFamily, expected);\n500:         if (family) addAttachedRow(family[row.split], top1Hit, attachedHit);\n501:       }\n502:       if (attached !== undefined) attachedRows[row.id] = attached;\n503:       if (row.row_type === 'typed' && result?.ambiguous === true && isBridgeOwnerPair(idToCommand, ids, expected)) {\n504:         sameOwnerAmbiguous.push(row.id);\n505:       }\n506: \n507:       const commandTally = expected === null ? undefined : perCommand.get(expected);\n508:       if (commandTally) {\n509:         if (row.row_type === 'typed') addRow(commandTally.typed, top1Hit, top3Hit);\n510:         else if (isPlainSplit) addRow(commandTally.plain[row.split], top1Hit, top3Hit);\n511:       }\n512: \n513:       rows[row.id] = { expected_command: expected, top: ids[0] ?? null, top1: top1Hit, top3: top3Hit, offered };\n514: \n515:       const isSlashVariant = row.row_type === 'slash-variant';\n516:       // Silence is the requirement for a near miss, a prompt that must not\n517:       // trigger a command, and a partial slash mention that names no command.\n518:       const wantsSilence = row.row_type === 'must-not-start'\n519:         || row.row_type === 'near-miss'\n520:         || (isSlashVariant && expected === null);\n521:       if (wantsSilence) {\n522:         falseOfferTotal += 1;\n523:         if (offered) falseOfferCount += 1;\n524:         if (attached !== undefined) falseOfferAttached += 1;\n525:       }\n526: \n527:       if (isSlashVariant) {\n528:         if (expected === null) {\n529:           slashPartialTotal += 1;\n530:           if (offered) slashOfferedOnPartial += 1;\n531:         } else {\n532:           slashNamedTotal += 1;\n533:           if (top3Hit) slashReachedTop3 += 1;\n534:         }\n535:       }\n536: \n537:       // A bridge offered below a skill is correct when the skill that outranked\n538:       // it is the bridge's own owner: the command stays reachable without the\n539:       // bridge displacing the skill the prompt actually asked for.\n540:       if (!idToCommand.has(ids[0])) {\n541:         const laterBridgeId = ids.slice(1).find((id) => idToCommand.has(id));\n542:         if (laterBridgeId !== undefined) {\n543:           agreementTotal += 1;\n544:           if (idToCommand.get(laterBridgeId).skillId === ids[0]) agreementCorrect += 1;\n545:         }\n546:       }\n547:     }\n548: \n549:     const commands = {};\n550:     for (const command of [...perCommand.keys()].sort()) {\n551:       commands[command] = perCommand.get(command);\n552:     }\n553: \n554:     const reachableCommands = new Set();\n555:     for (const value of idToCommand.values()) reachableCommands.add(value.command);\n556:     const unreachable = commandNames.filter((command) => !reachableCommands.has(command)).sort();\n557: \n558:     const plainByFamily = {};\n559:     for (const namespace of [...plainAttachedByFamily.keys()].sort()) {\n560:       const family = plainAttachedByFamily.get(namespace);\n561:       plainByFamily[namespace] = {\n562:         tune: attachedTallyJson(family.tune),\n563:         holdout: attachedTallyJson(family.holdout),\n564:       };\n565:     }\n566: \n567:     return {\n568:       schemaVersion: 1,\n569:       capturedAt,\n570:       capturedAtSha: readHeadSha(workspaceRoot),\n571:       corpusSha256: sha256File(CORPUS_JSONL),\n572:       bridgeCounts,\n573:       metrics: {\n574:         typed_recall_at_1: { correct: typed.top1, total: typed.rows },\n575:         typed_reach_top3: { correct: typed.top3, total: typed.rows },\n576:         plain_recall_at_1: {\n577:           tune: { correct: plain.tune.top1, total: plain.tune.rows },\n578:           holdout: { correct: plain.holdout.top1, total: plain.holdout.rows },\n579:         },\n580:         plain_reach_top3: {\n581:           tune: { correct: plain.tune.top3, total: plain.tune.rows },\n582:           holdout: { correct: plain.holdout.top3, total: plain.holdout.rows },\n583:         },\n584:         false_offer: { count: falseOfferCount, total: falseOfferTotal },\n585:         slash_variant: {\n586:           reached_top3: slashReachedTop3,\n587:           named_total: slashNamedTotal,\n588:           offered_on_partial: slashOfferedOnPartial,\n589:           partial_total: slashPartialTotal,\n590:         },\n591:         agreement: { correct: agreementCorrect, total: agreementTotal },\n592:       },\n593:       unreachable,\n594:       commands,\n595:       rows,\n596:       attachedMetrics: {\n597:         plain: {\n598:           tune: attachedTallyJson(plainAttached.tune),\n599:           holdout: attachedTallyJson(plainAttached.holdout),\n600:         },\n601:         plain_by_family: plainByFamily,\n602:         false_offer_attached: { count: falseOfferAttached, total: falseOfferTotal },\n603:         typed_same_owner_ambiguous: sameOwnerAmbiguous.sort(),\n604:         attachedRows,\n605:       },\n606:     };\n607:   } finally {\n608:     globalThis.Date = RealDate;\n609:     // Removal comes last, after every read that needs the directory has\n610:     // finished; `force` keeps an already-swept directory from turning a\n611:     // completed capture into a failure.\n612:     if (dbDir !== null) rmSync(dbDir, { recursive: true, force: true });\n613:   }\n614: }\n615: \n616: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n617: // 7. CLI ENTRY\n618: // ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n619: \n620: if (process.argv[1] && import.meta.url === pathToFileURL(resolve(process.argv[1])).href) {\n621:   rejectUnknownOptions(import.meta.url, ['--write', '--real-embedding']);\n622: \n623:   const realEmbedding = process.argv.includes('--real-embedding');\n624:   // The committed baseline has to be reproducible on a clean checkout, so it may\n625:   // only come from the fixture regime. A real-embedding run measures this machine's\n626:   // embedder and graph, which is a live probe rather than a ratchet.\n627:   if (realEmbedding && process.argv.includes('--write')) {\n628:     process.stderr.write('capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs: --write cannot be combined with --real-embedding; the committed baseline is captured under the fixture regime only\\n');\n629:     process.exit(2);\n630:   }\n631: \n632:   try {\n633:     const baseline = await scoreCommandCorpus({ realEmbedding });\n634:     const serialized = `${JSON.stringify(baseline, null, 2)}\\n`;\n635:     if (process.argv.includes('--write')) {\n636:       writeFileSync(OUTPUT_JSON, serialized, 'utf8');\n637:       console.log(`wrote baseline -> ${OUTPUT_JSON}`);\n638:     }\n639:     console.log(serialized);\n640:   } catch (error) {\n641:     process.stderr.write(`capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs: ${error.message}\\n`);\n642:     process.exit(2);\n643:   }\n644: }"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\":\"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime/scripts/routing-accuracy/capture-command-routing-baseline.mjs\"}"},"time":{"start":1794522575803,"end":1794522576358}}}}
